              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-domain-scanner/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Domain Scanner Output — Fantasy Fiction Writer\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nScanned a comprehensive ~31KB domain brief describing an autonomous multi-agent system for writing romantic fantasy fiction. The brief covers the full creative lifecycle from raw story concept through publication-ready chapters, with emphasis on series production, adversarial quality gates, and deeply subcategorized artifact management.\\n\\nThe domain is organized around a **9-phase creative pipeline** (Concept → World Building → Character Design → Plot Architecture → Prose Style → Drafting → Revision → Beta Reading → Polish/Delivery) with **7 cross-cutting concerns** that span multiple phases.\\n\\nSupporting invariants document (`.fractal-factory/invariants.md`) provides 28 non-negotiable invariants and 14 craft toolbox techniques.\\n\\n## Subdomain Table\\n\\n| ID | Name | Complexity | Est. Agents | Description |\\n|----|------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\\n| SD-001 | user-interaction | low | 1 | Guide agent: gathers inputs, validates, writes story-config.json, launches pipeline |\\n| SD-002 | concept-development | medium | 2 | Idea distillation, theme extraction, premise refinement, comp titles, craft profile |\\n| SD-003 | world-building | high | 4 | Geography, magic systems, politics, history, culture, flora/fauna, consistency bible |\\n| SD-004 | character-design | high | 3 | Protagonist profiles, romance arc, supporting cast, antagonists, voice guides, relationship web |\\n| SD-005 | plot-architecture | high | 3 | Story structure, chapter outlines, tension mapping, subplot tracking, dual-arc interleave |\\n| SD-006 | prose-style-development | medium | 2 | Voice calibration, style guide, tone palette, reference material analysis |\\n| SD-007 | chapter-drafting | high | 4 | Sequential chapter production with voice/style/continuity adherence, chapter hooks |\\n| SD-008 | revision-editing | high | 4 | Three-pass editing: developmental, line edit, copy edit, with full revision traceability |\\n| SD-009 | beta-reading-simulation | high | 6 | Five independent reviewer lenses: romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality |\\n| SD-010 | polish-delivery | medium | 2 | Final proofread, chapter summaries, series KB update, delivery packaging |\\n| SD-011 | adversarial-quality-gates | high | 4 | Per-phase adversarial auditors with kill-your-darlings mandate, severity-gated blocking |\\n| SD-012 | continuity-tracking | medium | 2 | Character locations, timeline, knowledge matrix, Chekhov's guns, cross-chapter facts |\\n| SD-013 | series-knowledge-management | high | 3 | Series KB lifecycle, book→series promotion, sequel contracts, cross-book consistency |\\n| SD-014 | artifact-management | medium | 2 | Subcategory decomposition, directory schema, per-book isolation, cross-references |\\n| SD-015 | genre-compliance | medium | 2 | Romantic fantasy rules, slow-burn pacing, HEA/HFN, trope execution, heat-level |\\n| SD-016 | craft-toolbox-framework | medium | 2 | Two-tier quality system, story craft profile selection, per-story tool adjustments |\\n| SD-017 | originality-assurance | low | 1 | Plagiarism prevention, transformative-only influence, originality self-audit |\\n\\n## Cross-Cutting Concerns\\n\\nSeven subdomains are identified as cross-cutting — they interact with multiple pipeline phases rather than being confined to a single phase:\\n\\n1. **Adversarial Quality Gates (SD-011)** — the most pervasive cross-cutting concern. Every creative phase (SD-002 through SD-007) must include an adversarial auditor as a sub-phase before completion. These auditors cross-reference against ALL prior phase artifacts, not just the current phase's output.\\n\\n2. **Continuity Tracking (SD-012)** — spans drafting (updated after every chapter), revision (verified against), beta reading (craft lens checks), and series management (extends across books).\\n\\n3. **Series Knowledge Management (SD-013)** — affects concept development (sequel loads series KB), all creative phases (cross-reference against series KB for sequels), polish/delivery (promotes new facts into series KB), and artifact management (directory schema).\\n\\n4. **Artifact Management (SD-014)** — affects every phase that produces output. Enforces subcategory decomposition, per-book directory isolation, and cross-referencing conventions.\\n\\n5. **Genre Compliance (SD-015)** — affects concept (genre promise), character design (both leads need agency), plot architecture (dual-arc interleave), drafting (heat-level adherence), and beta reading (romance reader lens).\\n\\n6. **Craft Toolbox Framework (SD-016)** — selected during concept/plotting, then enforced by adversarial auditors in all subsequent phases.\\n\\n7. **Originality Assurance (SD-017)** — affects prose style development (transformative-only influence from reference material), drafting (no plagiarism in prose), and beta reading (originality reader lens).\\n\\n## Gaps & Ambiguities\\n\\n1. **Pipeline orchestration**: The brief describes the pipeline phases but does not specify how the overall pipeline is orchestrated — whether a single session orchestrator dispatches phases sequentially, or whether there's a coordinator per phase with a top-level router. This is an architecture decision for the roster planner.\\n\\n2. **Parallelism within phases**: The brief implies sequential phase execution (e.g., \\\"outline before draft\\\"), but some sub-tasks within phases could potentially run in parallel (e.g., multiple worldbuilding sub-areas could be built concurrently). The extent of intra-phase parallelism is not specified.\\n\\n3. **Revision loop depth**: The brief says criticals must be addressed before phase completion, and beta reading findings must be resolved, but doesn't specify maximum retry counts for revision loops. Could potentially loop indefinitely.\\n\\n4. **Chapter count flexibility**: The brief mentions user-provided constraints for chapter count, but doesn't specify default ranges or how the system should decide chapter count when not specified.\\n\\n5. **Heat-level calibration**: The brief mentions heat-level as a constraint (fade-to-black through explicit) but doesn't provide detailed guidance on how each level affects prose generation — this is presumably domain knowledge the drafting agents need.\\n\\n6. **Interaction between adversarial gates and beta reading**: Both serve quality-assurance functions. The brief states beta reading should encounter \\\"far fewer issues\\\" due to phase gates, but doesn't specify whether beta reading can trigger re-entry into earlier phases or only revision/polish.\\n\\n## Estimated Total Agent Count\\n\\n| Category | Subdomains | Estimated Agents |\\n|----------|-----------|-----------------|\\n| Pipeline phases (SD-001 through SD-010) | 10 | 31 |\\n| Cross-cutting concerns (SD-011 through SD-017) | 7 | 16 |\\n| **Total** | **17** | **47** |\\n\\nThis estimate of **~47 agents** is well within the maxAgents=80 budget, leaving headroom for coordinators, the session orchestrator, and any specialists that emerge during detailed roster planning. The actual count may shift as the roster planner decides on coordinator/specialist splits and determines which cross-cutting concerns are implemented as dedicated agents vs. skills/instructions embedded in pipeline agents.\\n\"}"
