        "reasoning_text": "Now I have all the information I need. Let me systematically extract ALL invariants from both sources.\n\nFrom **invariants.md**, I have:\n1. **Part 1: Invariants** (I1-I28, including I14a, I14b, I14c) — these are explicit, high-confidence\n2. **Part 2: Craft Toolbox** (T1-T14) — these are selectable per-story techniques, but the framework itself is an invariant\n\nLet me also check the domain brief for implicit invariants not already covered.\n\nLet me catalog all invariants:\n\n### From invariants.md - Explicit Invariants (confidence 0.9-1.0):\n\n\nThe narrative foundation requires romantic fantasy with romance as the emotional core and a happy-for-now ending minimum, complete internal consistency across all worldbuilding elements, distinct character voices for each POV, and emotional beats that feel earned through proper buildup. I need to show emotions through action and dialogue rather than exposition, ensure significant plot elements either pay off or are flagged for future books, and weave exposition naturally into scenes rather than info-dumping.\n\nFor structure, I'm committing to outlining before drafting, maintaining a continuity document that updates after each chapter, writing chapters sequentially, and conducting multiple passes including developmental edits, line edits, consistency checks, and beta reads. Every revision needs to trace back to its prompt, and I'm building in verification steps to catch voice drift between POV chapters, cross-reference continuity claims, and maintain a prose quality floor that eliminates clichés, told emotions, and weak sentences.\n\nBeyond that, I need to ensure the world feels authentic with no anachronistic references, keep dialogue sounding natural and speech-like, vary pacing between high and low tension moments, escalate the romance arc through distinct stages, and give the antagonist comprehensible motivations. For originality, I'm holding myself to zero plagiarism, drawing only from abstract patterns rather than specific content from influences, and including originality checks in beta reading. Finally, every creative phase gets an adversarial audit, my style analysis pulls directly from provided inputs, all artifacts reference their upstream dependencies, and I'm building in safeguards against silent failures.\n\nNow I'm looking at the craft toolbox framework—I should select 5-8 binding tools per story from options like Scene-Sequel Structure, Scene Value Shifts, Five Commandments Per Scene, and Try-Fail Cycles at the scene level, plus Tone Contract and Stakes at the story level.\n\nI'm also seeing additional craft tools for escalation and structure like The Black Moment, Internal Romantic Resistance, Subtext in Dialogue, Sanderson's First Law, Kill Your Darlings, Dual-Arc Interleave, Tension Mapping, and Scene-Sequel MRU that could round out the selection.\n\nFrom the domain brief, I'm identifying two critical structural rules: the guide agent must be the sole user-facing entry point with all other agents operating autonomously, and story-config.json needs to be generated and confirmed with the user before the system launches.\n\nI'm also noting that the system should be designed for series production from the start even for standalone stories, the minimum viable input is just a story idea with everything else optional, and there's an append-mostly constraint on the series knowledge base where facts from earlier books can't be silently contradicted later.\n\nNow I'm looking at the workflow requirements for sequels—the system needs to generate a Story Craft Profile during the concept phase, critical findings must block phase completion, and all critical or major beta findings require a revision pass. The output needs to be publication-ready romantic fantasy prose, the system handles one story at a time, and there's a quality expectation around dual-POV narratives and the craft toolbox selection.\n\nI'm mapping these invariants across the subdomains to understand how each requirement flows through the system's architecture. Now I'm mapping the remaining integrity principles to their corresponding standards—principles like avoiding info-dumping and ensuring character agency connect to specific standards, while process-oriented principles like outline-before-draft and multi-pass review anchor the production workflow, with particular emphasis on voice consistency verification and revision traceability for quality control. Now I'm mapping out the remaining validation criteria—checking for transformative influence, auditing originality, setting up adversarial phase gates to catch issues early, and ensuring all artifacts cross-reference properly without silent failures across the entire scope.\n\nThe Craft Toolbox itself operates as a framework invariant where I select 5-8 tools per story and lock them in once chosen, but the individual tools T1-T14 should be recorded as quality invariants with lower confidence since they're conditionally applied depending on the task at hand.\n\nNow I'm identifying the implicit invariants embedded in the domain brief — things like the Guide Agent serving as the sole entry point, story configuration happening before pipeline execution, and the series-first design pattern that shapes how stories are structured. Each of these has a confidence level reflecting how clearly they emerge from the requirements. I'm mapping out the remaining constraints around narrative consistency—retcons need justification, sequels must resolve hanging threads, character voices stay consistent across books, magic systems expand rather than contradict, naming stays unique, and all critical beta findings get addressed. I'm tallying up the explicit invariants from the document: I1 through I9 are behavioral, I10 through I14c cover structural and workflow concerns, I15 through I19 address quality, I20 through I22 touch on behavioral and quality aspects, and I23 through I28 handle process and workflow—that gives me 31 total. Adding the 14 tools plus a framework rule from the Craft Toolbox and roughly 17 implicit invariants from the domain brief brings me to about 63 constraints overall. The instructions ask for thoroughness, so I should work with all of them rather than cherry-picking.\n\nNow I'm mapping these to classification categories: the narrative invariants are behavioral, the structural ones split between structural and workflow, quality invariants stay as quality, originality ones are behavioral, and process invariants mix workflow with structural. The Craft Toolbox framework rule becomes a workflow entry, while the individual tools function as quality standards once selected. I'm ready to build out the JSON structure with sequential IDs starting from INV-001. Mapping out the rest of the invariants from the document—I've got Show Don't Tell through No Anachronisms, with several verification-focused rules clustered together for the review and revision phases. Now I'm mapping out the craft techniques—scene-sequel structure, value shifts within scenes, the five commandments of storytelling, try-fail cycles to build momentum, maintaining a consistent tone contract with the reader, escalating stakes through the narrative, hitting that crucial black moment, exploring internal romantic resistance, layering subtext into dialogue, applying Sanderson's First Law for magic systems, being willing to cut unnecessary material, interleaving dual arcs, and tracking tension across the whole piece.\n\nI'm realizing this is going to require a substantial JSON update with all these invariants, so I'll write a Python script to read the current domain model, integrate all the new techniques systematically, and output the updated file to avoid manual errors.",
