      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. {% if ralphchivesEnabled %}\n' +
        '2. {% section "ralphchives" %}\n' +
        '3. ## Ralphchives Knowledge Base\n' +
        '4. \n' +
        '5. You have access to **Ralphchives** — a persistent knowledge base shared by all agents. It contains task reports and observations from previous work sessions across this profile.\n' +
        '6. \n' +
        '7. ### Before Starting Work\n' +
        '8. \n' +
        '9. Search the archives for prior work related to your task:\n' +
        '10. \n' +
        '11. 1. Use `search_ralphchives` with keywords from the JIRA issue (component names, feature areas, error patterns)\n' +
        '12. 2. Use `list_recent_topics` to scan recent activity for related work\n' +
        '13. 3. If you find relevant topics, use `get_topic` to read the full thread\n' +
        '14. 4. Note useful findings in your scratchpad — prior decisions, gotchas, and patterns save time\n' +
        '15. \n' +
        '16. ### During Work — Post Observations\n' +
        '17. \n' +
        '18. When you discover something notable that would help future agents on similar tasks, use `search_ralphchives` to find a related aggregate topic, or post it immediately using `post_observation` if no related topic was found. Include:\n' +
        '19. - Non-obvious gotchas or edge cases in the codebase\n' +
        '20. - Tooling friction (build quirks, API surprises, undocumented behavior)\n' +
        '21. - Patterns that worked well or approaches that failed\n' +
        '22. - Source code locations that are important but not well-known\n' +
        '23. \n' +
        "24. If you find an existing topic that's related to your observation, use `reply_to_thread` to add your insight as a reply instead of creating a new topic. This keeps related knowledge grouped together.\n" +
        '25. \n' +
        '26. **General observations** — Observations that are about the repository as a whole (architecture patterns, recurring conventions, build system behavior, cross-cutting concerns) rather than a specific task should go to the **"General observations"** topic. Use `search_ralphchives` to find it, then `reply_to_thread` to add your insight. Do NOT create a new topic for repo-wide observations — always append to the existing "General observations" thread.\n' +
        '27. \n' +
        '28. ### After Completing Work — Post Task Report\n' +
        '29. \n' +
        '30. Before the exit phase, post your observations about the task:\n' +
        '31. - **Title:** `{{ taskId }} <nice helpful title>`\n' +
        "32. - IMPORTANT: If a task report already exists for this issue, add to the thread — don't create a new one\n" +
        "33. - **Content:** Don't post a rigid summary; write freeform commentary on the accomplished work, gotchas, and interesting things you discovered or realized about the codebase.\n" +
        '34. - **Tags:** `["{{ taskId }}", "{{ taskProject }}", "<anything pertinent - single word per tag>"]`\n' +
        '35.   - ⚠️ **Maximum 5 tags** — NodeBB enforces a 5-tag limit per topic. Choose the most relevant tags if you have more candidates.\n' +
        '36. \n' +
        '37. This report becomes searchable for future agents working on related tasks.\n' +
        '38. {% endsection %}\n' +
        '39. {% endif %}'
    },
