      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. ---\n' +
        '2. name: ralph-callout-selection\n' +
        '3. description: "When to use each admonition type (tip, info, note, warning, key) in the Xperience docs. Use this skill when deciding which callout box to wrap content in — choosing the wrong severity confuses readers and desensitizes them to actual warnings. Covers the semantic meaning of each type and common mistakes."\n' +
        '4. ---\n' +
        '5. {% raw %}\n' +
        '6. \n' +
        '7. # Callout Selection Guide\n' +
        '8. \n' +
        '9. Each admonition type has a specific semantic meaning. Using the wrong one dilutes its impact — if everything is a warning, nothing is.\n' +
        '10. \n' +
        '11. ## Severity ladder (low → high)\n' +
        '12. \n' +
        '13. ### {% tip %} — Helpful shortcut\n' +
        "14. Optional productivity improvements. The reader's task succeeds without this information.\n" +
        '15. \n' +
        `16. **Use for:** keyboard shortcuts, time-saving alternatives, "you can also..." suggestions, best practices that aren't required.\n` +
        '17. \n' +
        '18. ```liquid\n' +
        '19. {% tip %}\n' +
        '20. You can also use the `dotnet new` CLI to create the project without Visual Studio.\n' +
        '21. {% endtip %}\n' +
        '22. ```\n' +
        '23. \n' +
        '24. ### {% info %} — Neutral context\n' +
        '25. Background information that helps understanding. Not actionable — just context.\n' +
        '26. \n' +
        '27. **Use for:** "this feature was added in version X," explanations of why something works a certain way, links to related topics, definitions.\n' +
        '28. \n' +
        '29. ```liquid\n' +
        '30. {% info %}\n' +
        '31. The consent management API uses the GDPR compliance framework introduced in version 28.\n' +
        '32. {% endinfo %}\n' +
        '33. ```\n' +
        '34. \n' +
        '35. ### {% note %} — Important clarification\n' +
        '36. Something the reader should know to avoid confusion. Often clarifies a common misconception or an unexpected behavior.\n' +
        '37. \n' +
        `38. **Use for:** behaviors that differ from expectations, prerequisites that aren't obvious, "this only applies when..." caveats.\n` +
        '39. \n' +
        '40. ```liquid\n' +
        '41. {% note %}\n' +
        '42. Workflow transitions are only triggered for pages — not for reusable content items.\n' +
        '43. {% endnote %}\n' +
        '44. ```\n' +
        '45. \n' +
        '46. ### {% warning %} — Potential problem\n' +
        '47. Something that could cause real issues if ignored — data loss, broken functionality, security implications.\n' +
        '48. \n' +
        '49. **Use for:** destructive operations, irreversible actions, known bugs, compatibility breaks, security considerations.\n' +
        '50. \n' +
        '51. ```liquid\n' +
        '52. {% warning %}\n' +
        '53. Deleting a content type permanently removes all content items of that type. This action cannot be undone.\n' +
        '54. {% endwarning %}\n' +
        '55. ```\n' +
        '56. \n' +
        '57. ### {% key %} — Critical / blocking\n' +
        '58. Absolutely essential information. The reader cannot proceed without this. Use very sparingly.\n' +
        '59. \n' +
        '60. **Use for:** licensing requirements that block functionality, hard prerequisites, critical security constraints.\n' +
        '61. \n' +
        '62. ```liquid\n' +
        '63. {% key %}\n' +
        '64. You must enable the SaaS deployment feature in your license before following these steps.\n' +
        '65. {% endkey %}\n' +
        '66. ```\n' +
        '67. \n' +
        '68. ## Common mistakes\n' +
        '69. \n' +
        '70. | Mistake | Fix |'
    },
