      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. ---\n' +
        '2. name: vscode-extension-performance\n' +
        '3. description: Use this skill when working on VS Code extension startup cost, activation events, extension host performance, bundling, lazy registration, watch/build scripts, `.vscodeignore`, or when evaluating whether an extension loads too much work too early. Also use when modifying `package.json` activation events, bundler config, extension entry points, or performance-sensitive provider registration.\n' +
        '4. ---\n' +
        '5. \n' +
        '6. # VS Code Extension Performance\n' +
        '7. \n' +
        '8. Guidance for keeping VS Code extensions fast to activate, cheap to load, and lightweight to package.\n' +
        '9. \n' +
        '10. Use this skill for:\n' +
        '11. - startup and activation-cost reviews\n' +
        '12. - deciding between eager and lazy registration\n' +
        '13. - activation event selection in `package.json`\n' +
        '14. - bundling and package-size work\n' +
        '15. - extension host performance regressions\n' +
        '16. \n' +
        '17. ## Core Principles\n' +
        '18. \n' +
        '19. 1. **Activate only when needed**\n' +
        '20.    Prefer specific activation events over broad startup activation.\n' +
        '21. \n' +
        '22. 2. **Do not front-load work into `activate()`**\n' +
        '23.    Keep activation focused on registration and lightweight initialization.\n' +
        '24. \n' +
        '25. 3. **Bundle for install and load performance**\n' +
        '26.    Bundling reduces file-count overhead and improves load time, especially for web-compatible extensions.\n' +
        '27. \n' +
        '28. 4. **Type-check separately from bundling when needed**\n' +
        '29.    Bundlers such as esbuild strip types but do not replace real type checking.\n' +
        '30. \n' +
        '31. 5. **Publish only runtime artifacts**\n' +
        '32.    Exclude source and dev-only files from the VSIX using `.vscodeignore` or equivalent packaging rules.\n' +
        '33. \n' +
        '34. ## Activation Guidance\n' +
        '35. \n' +
        '36. - Prefer narrow activation events such as `onLanguage:<id>`, `onCommand:<id>`, `onView:<id>`, or `workspaceContains:<glob>`\n' +
        '37. - Avoid `*` unless no narrower combination works\n' +
        '38. - Avoid generic `onLanguage` unless the extension truly needs to activate before any language-specific work\n' +
        '39. - Treat `onStartupFinished` as lower-risk than `*`, but still justify it explicitly\n' +
        '40. \n' +
        '41. ## Runtime Guidance\n' +
        '42. \n' +
        '43. - Register providers, commands, and listeners first; defer expensive scanning or indexing\n' +
        '44. - Avoid large synchronous workspace scans in `activate()`\n' +
        '45. - Do not do heavyweight parsing for unopened files unless explicitly needed\n' +
        '46. - Cache derived data only when invalidation is clear and cheap\n' +
        '47. - Prefer incremental updates over full recomputation when documents change\n' +
        '48. \n' +
        '49. ## Bundling Guidance\n' +
        '50. \n' +
        '51. - Bundle the extension entry point into a single runtime artifact when possible\n' +
        '52. - Exclude the `vscode` module from the bundle\n' +
        '53. - Keep source maps for development builds, not necessarily for production packaging\n' +
        '54. - If using esbuild, still run `tsc --noEmit` for type safety\n' +
        '55. - If using webpack, treat critical dependency warnings as real bundle risks, not harmless noise\n' +
        '56. \n' +
        '57. ## Packaging Guidance\n' +
        '58. \n' +
        '59. - Keep runtime output in `dist/` or another explicit package target\n' +
        '60. - Exclude `src/`, transient build output, and unnecessary `node_modules` content from published packages\n' +
        '61. - Ensure `main` points at the bundled runtime artifact when bundling is introduced\n' +
        '62. \n' +
        '63. ## Review Checklist\n' +
        '64. \n' +
        '65. When reviewing performance-sensitive extension changes, check:\n' +
        '66. - Did activation events become broader?\n' +
        '67. - Did `activate()` gain synchronous heavy work?\n' +
        '68. - Did a new provider or watcher get registered globally when it could be language-scoped?\n' +
        '69. - Did bundling or packaging configuration regress?\n' +
        '70. - Did a build change increase package complexity without a clear runtime benefit?'
    },
