      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. ---\n' +
        '2. name: test-structure-patterns\n' +
        `3. description: "Guide for structuring test files, naming tests, using Given/When/Then, organizing tests by behavior instead of by code structure, and understanding what counts as 'observable output'. Use this skill when organizing a test file, deciding how to group tests, naming test cases, structuring setup/act/assert blocks, or when tests mirror code structure too closely. Also triggers on: test file organization, describe blocks, test naming, arranging test suites, Given-When-Then."\n` +
        '4. ---\n' +
        '5. \n' +
        '6. # Test Structure Patterns\n' +
        '7. \n' +
        '8. ## Organize by Behavior, Not by Code\n' +
        '9. \n' +
        '10. Avoid the trap of mirroring your source code structure in tests — one describe block per class, one test per method. This couples tests to implementation structure and leads to meaningless test names.\n' +
        '11. \n' +
        "12. Instead, organize tests around **behaviors and use cases** — what does the system do from a caller's perspective?\n" +
        '13. \n' +
        '14. ```ts\n' +
        '15. // Good — organized by behavior\n' +
        '16. describe("RepoSyncHook", () => {\n' +
        '17.   it("syncs repo to default branch and creates task branch", ...);\n' +
        '18.   it("switches to existing branch on revision", ...);\n' +
        '19.   it("uses custom source branch from trigger params", ...);\n' +
        '20.   it("throws when credentials are missing", ...);\n' +
        '21. });\n' +
        '22. \n' +
        '23. // Bad — organized by method internals\n' +
        '24. describe("RepoSyncHook", () => {\n' +
        '25.   describe("execute", () => {\n' +
        '26.     it("calls git fetch", ...);\n' +
        '27.     it("calls git checkout", ...);\n' +
        '28.     it("calls git reset --hard", ...);\n' +
        '29.     it("calls mkdirSync", ...);\n' +
        '30.   });\n' +
        '31. });\n' +
        '32. ```\n' +
        '33. \n' +
        '34. The first version tells you what the hook *does*. The second tells you what it *calls* — which is useless as documentation and fragile under refactoring.\n' +
        '35. \n' +
        '36. ## Given / When / Then\n' +
        '37. \n' +
        "38. Structure each test in three clear phases. You don't need comments labeling them — the structure should be visually obvious through whitespace:\n" +
        '39. \n' +
        '40. ```ts\n' +
        '41. it("rejects registration with duplicate email", async () => {\n' +
        '42.   // Given — set up preconditions\n' +
        '43.   const existingUser = makeUser({ email: "taken@example.com" });\n' +
        '44.   await store.save(existingUser);\n' +
        '45. \n' +
        '46.   // When — execute the action\n' +
        '47.   const result = await service.register({ email: "taken@example.com", name: "New" });\n' +
        '48. \n' +
        '49.   // Then — verify outcome\n' +
        '50.   expect(result.success).toBe(false);\n' +
        '51.   expect(result.error).toBe("email_taken");\n' +
        '52. });\n' +
        '53. ```\n' +
        '54. \n' +
        `55. Keep each phase short. If the "Given" section is 20 lines of mock setup, that's a signal to use factories or fakes (see **test-mocking-strategy** skill).\n` +
        '56. \n' +
        '57. ## What Counts as Observable Output\n' +
        '58. \n' +
        '59. This is the key distinction for deciding what to assert on:\n' +
        '60. \n' +
        "61. | Observable (assert on this)                    | Internal (don't assert on this)                |\n" +
        '62. |------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|\n' +
        '63. | Return value of a public method                | Which private methods were called              |\n' +
        '64. | State change visible via public getters/API    | Order of internal operations                   |\n' +
        '65. | Side effect: record written to DB/file         | Which specific query or write path was used    |\n' +
        '66. | Side effect: message published to a queue      | How the payload was assembled internally       |\n' +
        '67. | Error/exception thrown for invalid input       | Which validation helper caught it              |\n' +
        '68. | HTTP response status + body                    | Which internal service handled the request     |\n' +
        '69. | Arguments passed to an architectural boundary  | Arguments passed between internal collaborators|\n' +
        '70. '
    },
