      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. ---\n' +
        '2. name: vscode-grammar-and-scopes\n' +
        '3. description: Use this skill when working on VS Code TextMate grammars, injection grammars, syntax highlighting, embedded languages, token scopes, fenced code blocks, bracket matching scopes, or when changing files under `grammars/`, `syntaxes/`, or grammar contribution points in `package.json`. Also use when reviewing scope names, `embeddedLanguages`, `tokenTypes`, `injectTo`, `injectionSelector`, or debugging highlighting with the scope inspector.\n' +
        '4. ---\n' +
        '5. \n' +
        '6. # VS Code Grammar And Scopes\n' +
        '7. \n' +
        '8. Guidance for changing or reviewing VS Code grammar-based language features.\n' +
        '9. \n' +
        '10. Use this skill for:\n' +
        '11. - TextMate grammar files and injection grammars\n' +
        '12. - Syntax highlighting regressions\n' +
        '13. - Embedded language behavior inside strings, comments, or fenced blocks\n' +
        '14. - Bracket matching, comment toggling, or snippet behavior caused by scope changes\n' +
        '15. - Scope naming and theme compatibility reviews\n' +
        '16. \n' +
        '17. ## Core Model\n' +
        '18. \n' +
        '19. VS Code grammar highlighting has two layers:\n' +
        '20. - **TextMate tokenization** decides scopes using regex-based grammar rules\n' +
        '21. - **Semantic tokens** may add extra highlighting later, but they do not replace a broken grammar\n' +
        '22. \n' +
        "23. For this repo, grammar work must preserve the extension's definition-driven architecture while also preserving correct editor behavior for KFM tags and embedded content.\n" +
        '24. \n' +
        '25. ## Rules\n' +
        '26. \n' +
        '27. 1. **Prefer existing standard scopes**\n' +
        '28.    Use common TextMate scopes that themes already understand. Do not invent custom scope names when a standard parent scope already fits.\n' +
        '29. \n' +
        '30. 2. **Treat grammar changes as editor-behavior changes**\n' +
        '31.    A scope change can affect more than colors. It may change bracket matching, comment behavior, snippet applicability, and embedded-language features.\n' +
        '32. \n' +
        '33. 3. **Keep grammar edits local and explicit**\n' +
        '34.    Prefer small repository includes and targeted injection selectors over broad regex that changes unrelated text.\n' +
        '35. \n' +
        '36. 4. **Use injection grammars carefully**\n' +
        '37.    Injection grammars should target the narrowest possible scope using `injectTo` and `injectionSelector`.\n' +
        '38. \n' +
        '39. 5. **Use embedded languages when the editor should switch behavior**\n' +
        '40.    If embedded content should get its own comments, brackets, snippets, or language features, map it with `embeddedLanguages`.\n' +
        '41. \n' +
        '42. 6. **Reset token content mode for embedded code**\n' +
        '43.    When embedded code lives inside strings or comments, prefer wrapping it in a `meta.embedded.*` scope. If that is not possible, use `tokenTypes` so VS Code treats it as code instead of string/comment content.\n' +
        '44. \n' +
        '45. 7. **Review unmatched begin/end rules for end-of-file behavior**\n' +
        '46.    TextMate grammars can leave unmatched regions active until end-of-document. Check that incomplete tags or fences degrade safely.\n' +
        '47. \n' +
        '48. ## What To Check\n' +
        '49. \n' +
        '50. ### Scope design\n' +
        '51. \n' +
        '52. - Does the most specific scope describe the token accurately?\n' +
        '53. - Are parent scopes still meaningful for theming?\n' +
        '54. - Is the change compatible with common theme expectations?\n' +
        '55. - Did the change accidentally broaden a scope that used to be narrow?\n' +
        '56. \n' +
        '57. ### Injection grammars\n' +
        '58. \n' +
        '59. - Is `injectTo` limited to the correct host scope?\n' +
        '60. - Is `injectionSelector` narrow enough to avoid unrelated matches?\n' +
        '61. - If precedence matters, is left-injection (`L:`) or equivalent behavior chosen intentionally?\n' +
        '62. \n' +
        '63. ### Embedded languages\n' +
        '64. \n' +
        '65. - Is `embeddedLanguages` configured when embedded content should behave like another language?\n' +
        '66. - Is a `meta.embedded.*` scope used so bracket matching and commenting work correctly?\n' +
        '67. - If not, is `tokenTypes` used to reset content mode where needed?\n' +
        '68. \n' +
        '69. ### Brackets and comments\n' +
        '70. '
    },
