      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. ---\n' +
        '2. name: ralph-task-planning\n' +
        '3. description: "Core planning skill for the Ralph docs planner sub-agent. Use this skill whenever research or revision feedback must be broken into headless execution tasks for the writer, when a documentation change needs to be decomposed into ordered task files, when you need to choose task boundaries, dependencies, deferred work, or reviewer focus sections, or when the writer/reviewer loop runs per task instead of across one monolithic implementation batch."\n' +
        '4. ---\n' +
        '5. \n' +
        '6. # Ralph Task Planning\n' +
        '7. \n' +
        '8. Use this skill to turn research artifacts or revision feedback into an ordered, machine-friendly task plan for a fully headless Ralph docs run.\n' +
        '9. \n' +
        '10. This skill is for the **planner**, not the writer. Your job is to decide:\n' +
        '11. \n' +
        '12. - what the tasks are\n' +
        '13. - what order they should run in\n' +
        '14. - what each task owns\n' +
        '15. - what should be deferred instead of silently folded into another task\n' +
        '16. - what each reviewer should pay attention to for that task\n' +
        '17. \n' +
        '18. Read the bundled references before planning.\n' +
        '19. \n' +
        '20. ## Read These References\n' +
        '21. \n' +
        '22. | File | Purpose |\n' +
        '23. |---|---|\n' +
        '24. | `references/task-boundaries.md` | How to choose task granularity, ownership, and dependencies |\n' +
        '25. | `references/task-files.md` | The required headless task-file structure and `tasks.json` expectations |\n' +
        '26. | `references/revision-mode.md` | How to plan revision work from reviewer findings and preserve already approved work |\n' +
        '27. \n' +
        '28. ## Default Process\n' +
        '29. \n' +
        '30. 1. Inventory all available research or revision artifacts.\n' +
        '31. 2. Group work by documentation neighborhood, file ownership, and dependency chain rather than by the order the researcher wrote things down.\n' +
        '32. 3. Split risky mixed work into separate tasks when docs edits, navigation/frontmatter work, codesamples work, or release notes would otherwise be tangled together.\n' +
        '33. 4. Emit explicit dependencies only when one task must land before another.\n' +
        '34. 5. Mark genuinely out-of-scope work as follow-up instead of hiding it inside a task.\n' +
        '35. 6. Write task files that a fresh writer can execute without chat history.\n' +
        '36. 7. Include reviewer-focus notes so each reviewer can audit the right things per task.\n' +
        '37. \n' +
        '38. ## Rules\n' +
        '39. \n' +
        '40. - Plan for **headless execution**. Do not write manual checkpoints, commit instructions, or user handoffs.\n' +
        '41. - Prefer **small, reviewable tasks** over large implementation batches.\n' +
        '42. - Keep `_guides` out of scope for Ralph docs planning.\n' +
        '43. - Minimize file overlap between tasks.\n' +
        '44. - Do not invent work that is unsupported by research or revision findings.\n' +
        '45. - If a task would be too vague to assign to a fresh writer, split it further.\n' +
        '46. - If multiple tasks touch the same file, explain why and make the dependency explicit.\n' +
        '47. \n' +
        '48. ## Expected Outcome\n' +
        '49. \n' +
        '50. After using this skill, you should have:\n' +
        '51. \n' +
        '52. - one ordered `tasks.json`\n' +
        '53. - one markdown task file per execution task\n' +
        '54. - clear deferred-work notes\n' +
        '55. - reviewer-focus guidance per task\n' +
        '56. - a plan that supports writer -> reviewer loops on one task at a time'
    },
