              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-asset-auditor/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Asset Auditor Report — Romantic Fantasy Writer\\n\\n**Agent:** fractal-factory-asset-auditor  \\n**Iteration:** 1  \\n**Timestamp:** 2026-03-15T20:15:00Z\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nScanned the ralph-orchestrator repository for assets reusable by the romantic-fantasy-writer agent system. Examined **6 shared skill categories** (~50 skills), **9 MCP servers**, **7 agent include files**, **2 personality templates**, **4 post-hook patterns**, **2 agent profiles**, **28 fractal factory exemplar agents**, and **6 hook scripts**.\\n\\n**Result:** 25 assets catalogued as relevant. The vast majority of code-specific skills (TypeScript, Ruby, Jekyll, .NET, VSCode) are irrelevant to creative fiction writing. However, the **infrastructure patterns** — artifact handoff contracts, workflow phasing, multi-reviewer panels, revision loops, personality templates — are highly transferable.\\n\\n## Scan Statistics\\n\\n| Category Scanned | Items Found | Relevant | Irrelevant |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Shared Skills (domain/) | 20 | 3 | 17 |\\n| Shared Skills (integrations/) | 6 | 0 | 6 |\\n| Shared Skills (tasks/) | 6 | 3 | 3 |\\n| Shared Skills (workflow/) | 15 | 5 | 10 |\\n| Shared Skills (xperience/) | 2 | 0 | 2 |\\n| Agent Includes (core) | 7 | 3 | 4 |\\n| Agent Includes (personality/) | 2 | 2 | 0 |\\n| Agent Includes (post-hooks/) | 4 | 3 | 1 |\\n| Agent Includes (ralph-docs/) | 14 | 2 | 12 |\\n| MCP Servers | 9 | 3 | 6 |\\n| Profiles | 2 | 1 | 1 |\\n| Fractal Factory Agents | 28 | 2 | 26 |\\n| Hook Scripts | 7 | 1 | 6 |\\n| **Total** | **122** | **28 assessed → 25 catalogued** | **94** |\\n\\n## Reusability Classification\\n\\n### Direct Reuse (3 assets) — Mount as-is\\n\\n| ID | Asset | Why Direct |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| ASSET-001 | agent-as-function-contract | Universal artifact handoff pattern — status.json, output.md versioning, manifest.json audit trail. Every agent in the fiction system needs this. |\\n| ASSET-002 | operational-rules | Routing constraints (read only status.json, wait for subagents, block correctly). Domain-agnostic. |\\n| ASSET-003 | prompt-security | Prompt injection defense, data exfiltration prevention. User-provided story prompts are untrusted input — same security model applies. |\\n\\n### Adaptable (8 assets) — Useful with domain-specific modifications\\n\\n| ID | Asset | Adaptation Needed |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| ASSET-004 | personality-ralph | Replace technical explorer persona with creative writer persona. Keep the structural pattern (voice, communication style, behavioral constraints). |\\n| ASSET-005 | personality-malph | Replace code reviewer persona with literary critic/beta reader personas. Keep the adversarial thoroughness pattern. Multiple adaptations needed for different review lenses. |\\n| ASSET-006 | ralph-workflow-phased-pipeline | Replace 8 documentation phases with fiction phases (concept→worldbuild→character→plot→draft→revise→beta→polish). Keep state.md tracking and phase transition routing. |\\n| ASSET-007 | malph-review-panel-workflow | Replace technical/style/IA reviewers with romance/fantasy/craft/continuity reviewers. Keep the mechanical verdict aggregation from status.json. |\\n| ASSET-008 | malph-verdict-aggregation | Adapt verdict delivery for fiction context — aggregate beta reader feedback into prioritized revision directives instead of PR comments. |\\n| ASSET-009 | ralph-task-planning | Adapt task decomposition for fiction: convert plot outlines into chapter-level writing tasks with scene dependencies, or revision feedback into ordered editing passes. |\\n| ASSET-014 | web-fetch-mcp | Could fetch reference fiction analysis, genre convention resources, or name/culture databases during worldbuilding. Needs story-appropriate URL allowlisting. |\\n| ASSET-024 | revision-workflow-pattern | 4-phase revision loop (Setup→Fix→Verify→Deliver) maps to fiction revision cycle. Replace \\\"defect\\\" terminology with \\\"revision directive\\\" terminology. |\\n\\n### Reference Only (14 assets) — Patterns to learn from\\n\\n| ID | Asset | What Pattern to Extract |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| ASSET-010 | ralph-style-guide-review | Style enforcement checklist pattern → adapt for prose voice consistency, POV discipline, tone palette |\\n| ASSET-011 | run-analyzer-pattern | Per-agent quality analysis → adapt for per-chapter drafting quality tracking |\\n| ASSET-012 | agent-improver-pattern | Self-improvement loop → adapt for iterative style guide refinement based on revision feedback |\\n| ASSET-013 | subagent-mapper-pattern | Provenance tracking → which agent produced which chapter/artifact |\\n| ASSET-015 | discord-hitl-mcp | Human-in-the-loop → guide agent could use similar mechanism for story direction questions |\\n| ASSET-016 | ralphchives-knowledge-base | Persistent knowledge store → series knowledge management across volumes |\\n| ASSET-017 | profile-configuration-pattern | Profile config structure → how to wire MCP servers, skills, and quality gates |\\n| ASSET-018 | behavior-testing-skill | Behavior-focused assertions → fiction quality measured by observable narrative properties |\\n| ASSET-019 | fractal-factory-pipeline-exemplar | 7-pass pipeline with coordinators/specialists → structural template for fiction pipeline |\\n| ASSET-020 | fractal-factory-gap-hunter-pattern | Adversarial gap hunting → continuity auditors searching for plot holes and contradictions |\\n| ASSET-021 | ralph-documentation-syntax | Structured document formatting → chapter/scene formatting conventions |\\n| ASSET-022 | ralph-write-release-notes | Structured writing workflow → synopsis and series bible entry generation |\\n| ASSET-023 | audit-hooks-system | Execution audit trail → pipeline provenance and quality gate history |\\n| ASSET-025 | vscode-multi-model-review-panel | Multi-model review with full checklist → multi-lens beta reading simulation |\\n\\n## Key Recommendations\\n\\n### 1. Adopt the Agent-as-Function Contract Wholesale\\nThe artifact handoff pattern (ASSET-001–003) is domain-agnostic infrastructure. The fiction system should use identical status.json routing, output.md versioning, and manifest.json audit trails. No adaptation needed.\\n\\n### 2. Adapt the Multi-Reviewer Panel for Beta Reading\\nThe Malph review panel (ASSET-007, ASSET-025) is the closest existing pattern to the simulated multi-lens beta reading described in the domain brief. Each \\\"beta reader\\\" lens (romance reader, fantasy worldbuilding critic, craft auditor, continuity checker) maps to a specialized reviewer with its own checklist — exactly how the Malph panel dispatches technical/style/IA reviewers.\\n\\n### 3. Create New Personality Templates from the Existing Pattern\\nThe Ralph/Malph personality split (ASSET-004, ASSET-005) demonstrates how to give agents distinct voices while maintaining behavioral constraints. The fiction system needs multiple distinct personalities: the enthusiastic drafter, the meticulous worldbuilder, the merciless continuity auditor, the empathetic romance reader, etc.\\n\\n### 4. Adapt the Phased Workflow for Fiction Generation\\nThe 8-phase documentation workflow (ASSET-006) and 4-phase revision loop (ASSET-024) provide the structural skeleton. The fiction pipeline's phases (concept→worldbuild→character→plot→style→draft→revise→beta→polish) are structurally analogous but need domain-specific state tracking (e.g., tracking which scenes are drafted vs. revised vs. polished).\\n\\n### 5. Consider a Knowledge Base for Series Management\\nThe Ralphchives pattern (ASSET-016) — a persistent, searchable knowledge base backed by a forum engine — is a reference pattern for the series knowledge management subdomain (SD-013). A simpler filesystem-based approach may suffice for v1, but the search/retrieval patterns are instructive.\\n\\n### 6. Most Code-Specific Skills Are Irrelevant\\nTypeScript best practices, Ruby gems, Jekyll site structure, .NET code samples, ADO PR workflows, JIRA integration, VSCode grammars — none of these apply to creative fiction writing. The fiction system needs its own domain skills (worldbuilding, character psychology, plot structure, prose style, genre conventions) built from scratch.\\n\\n## Assets NOT Catalogued (Notable Exclusions)\\n\\n- **codegraphcontext MCP** — code graph analysis is irrelevant to fiction\\n- **playwright MCP** — browser automation is irrelevant to fiction\\n- **ado MCP** — Azure DevOps integration is irrelevant\\n- **jira-kentico MCP** — JIRA integration is irrelevant\\n- **microsoft-docs MCP** — Microsoft Learn search is irrelevant\\n- **All xperience skills** — Kentico Xperience product knowledge is irrelevant\\n- **All codesamples skills** — .NET code sample management is irrelevant\\n- **All devralph skills** — Ruby/Gulp/Jekyll infrastructure is irrelevant\\n- **All vscode skills** — VS Code extension development is irrelevant\\n- **TypeScript/code review skills** — software code quality is irrelevant\\n\"}"
