      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '1. # Domain Scanner Output — Fantasy Fiction Writer\n' +
        '2. \n' +
        '3. ## Summary\n' +
        '4. \n' +
        '5. Scanned a comprehensive ~31KB domain brief describing an autonomous multi-agent system for writing romantic fantasy fiction. The brief covers the full creative lifecycle from raw story concept through publication-ready chapters, with emphasis on series production, adversarial quality gates, and deeply subcategorized artifact management.\n' +
        '6. \n' +
        '7. The domain is organized around a **9-phase creative pipeline** (Concept → World Building → Character Design → Plot Architecture → Prose Style → Drafting → Revision → Beta Reading → Polish/Delivery) with **7 cross-cutting concerns** that span multiple phases.\n' +
        '8. \n' +
        '9. Supporting invariants document (`.fractal-factory/invariants.md`) provides 28 non-negotiable invariants and 14 craft toolbox techniques.\n' +
        '10. \n' +
        '11. ## Subdomain Table\n' +
        '12. \n' +
        '13. | ID | Name | Complexity | Est. Agents | Description |\n' +
        '14. |----|------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n' +
        '15. | SD-001 | user-interaction | low | 1 | Guide agent: gathers inputs, validates, writes story-config.json, launches pipeline |\n' +
        '16. | SD-002 | concept-development | medium | 2 | Idea distillation, theme extraction, premise refinement, comp titles, craft profile |\n' +
        '17. | SD-003 | world-building | high | 4 | Geography, magic systems, politics, history, culture, flora/fauna, consistency bible |\n' +
        '18. | SD-004 | character-design | high | 3 | Protagonist profiles, romance arc, supporting cast, antagonists, voice guides, relationship web |\n' +
        '19. | SD-005 | plot-architecture | high | 3 | Story structure, chapter outlines, tension mapping, subplot tracking, dual-arc interleave |\n' +
        '20. | SD-006 | prose-style-development | medium | 2 | Voice calibration, style guide, tone palette, reference material analysis |\n' +
        '21. | SD-007 | chapter-drafting | high | 4 | Sequential chapter production with voice/style/continuity adherence, chapter hooks |\n' +
        '22. | SD-008 | revision-editing | high | 4 | Three-pass editing: developmental, line edit, copy edit, with full revision traceability |\n' +
        '23. | SD-009 | beta-reading-simulation | high | 6 | Five independent reviewer lenses: romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality |\n' +
        '24. | SD-010 | polish-delivery | medium | 2 | Final proofread, chapter summaries, series KB update, delivery packaging |\n' +
        '25. | SD-011 | adversarial-quality-gates | high | 4 | Per-phase adversarial auditors with kill-your-darlings mandate, severity-gated blocking |\n' +
        "26. | SD-012 | continuity-tracking | medium | 2 | Character locations, timeline, knowledge matrix, Chekhov's guns, cross-chapter facts |\n" +
        '27. | SD-013 | series-knowledge-management | high | 3 | Series KB lifecycle, book→series promotion, sequel contracts, cross-book consistency |\n' +
        '28. | SD-014 | artifact-management | medium | 2 | Subcategory decomposition, directory schema, per-book isolation, cross-references |\n' +
        '29. | SD-015 | genre-compliance | medium | 2 | Romantic fantasy rules, slow-burn pacing, HEA/HFN, trope execution, heat-level |\n' +
        '30. | SD-016 | craft-toolbox-framework | medium | 2 | Two-tier quality system, story craft profile selection, per-story tool adjustments |\n' +
        '31. | SD-017 | originality-assurance | low | 1 | Plagiarism prevention, transformative-only influence, originality self-audit |\n' +
        '32. \n' +
        '33. ## Cross-Cutting Concerns\n' +
        '34. \n' +
        '35. Seven subdomains are identified as cross-cutting — they interact with multiple pipeline phases rather than being confined to a single phase:\n' +
        '36. \n' +
        "37. 1. **Adversarial Quality Gates (SD-011)** — the most pervasive cross-cutting concern. Every creative phase (SD-002 through SD-007) must include an adversarial auditor as a sub-phase before completion. These auditors cross-reference against ALL prior phase artifacts, not just the current phase's output.\n" +
        '38. \n' +
        '39. 2. **Continuity Tracking (SD-012)** — spans drafting (updated after every chapter), revision (verified against), beta reading (craft lens checks), and series management (extends across books).\n' +
        '40. \n' +
        '41. 3. **Series Knowledge Management (SD-013)** — affects concept development (sequel loads series KB), all creative phases (cross-reference against series KB for sequels), polish/delivery (promotes new facts into series KB), and artifact management (directory schema).\n' +
        '42. \n' +
        '43. 4. **Artifact Management (SD-014)** — affects every phase that produces output. Enforces subcategory decomposition, per-book directory isolation, and cross-referencing conventions.\n' +
        '44. \n' +
        '45. 5. **Genre Compliance (SD-015)** — affects concept (genre promise), character design (both leads need agency), plot architecture (dual-arc interleave), drafting (heat-level adherence), and beta reading (romance reader lens).\n' +
        '46. \n' +
        '47. 6. **Craft Toolbox Framework (SD-016)** — selected during concept/plotting, then enforced by adversarial auditors in all subsequent phases.\n' +
        '48. \n' +
        '49. 7. **Originality Assurance (SD-017)** — affects prose style development (transformative-only influence from reference material), drafting (no plagiarism in prose), and beta reading (originality reader lens).\n' +
        '50. '
    },
