      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "Now I have comprehensive information. Let me provide the complete analysis:\n\n---\n\n## Ralph Agent Profile Analysis\n\nI've analyzed both profile families and the fractal-factory meta-orchestrator. Here's the complete breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### **1. RALPH-PROFILE PATTERN (Exemplars: ralph-docs & ralph-vscode)**\n\nThese are **task-oriented profiles** that route live JIRA/ADO work through specialized agents.\n\n#### **Agent Organization & Roles**\n\n**ralph-docs** (25 agents):\n- **Orchestrator**: `ralph` (router, pure state machine)\n- **Research**: `ralph-researcher`, `ralph-coder`\n- **Planning**: `ralph-planner`\n- **Implementation**: `ralph-writer`\n- **Review**: 6 reviewers (3 Claude + 3 GPT: technical, style, IA)\n- **Delivery**: `ralph-scribe`\n- **Secondary**: `malph` (review orchestrator), `scientist` (analysis), `stacky` (fullstack dev), specialized variants\n\n**ralph-vscode** (16 agents):\n- **Orchestrator**: `ralph`\n- **Analysis**: `ralph-analyst` (+ 2 explorer helpers: robinson, vasco)\n- **Planning**: `ralph-planner`\n- **Implementation**: `ralph-coder`\n- **Review**: `ralph-reviewer`\n- **Delivery**: `ralph-scribe`\n- **Secondary**: `malph` (review orchestrator), `scientist` (meta-analysis)\n\n#### **profile.json Structure**\n\nBoth follow identical **variant-based routing**:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"repo\": \"target-repo-path\",\n  \"dataSource\": \"jira\",\n  \"vcsProvider\": \"ado\",\n  \"cli\": \"copilot\",\n  \"mcpServers\": [...],  // External integrations (JIRA, ADO, Ralphchives, etc.)\n  \"variants\": [\n    {\n      \"match\": {\n        \"projects\": [\"DOC\", \"DF\"],\n        \"statuses\": [\"To Do\"],\n        \"commentTrigger\": \"@Ralph\"\n      },\n      \"beforeAgent\": { \"targetStatus\": \"In Progress\" },\n      \"afterAgent\": { \"targetStatus\": \"Ready for Review\" },\n      \"stages\": [\n        {\n          \"agent\": \"ralph.ralph\",\n          \"role\": \"primary\",\n          \"skills\": [...]  // Domain-specific capabilities\n        }\n      ],\n      \"postTaskHooks\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"run-analysis\",\n          \"stages\": [{ \"agent\": \"ralph.scientist\", ... }]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Key elements:**\n- `match`: Trigger conditions (status, project, comment mention)\n- `stages`: Sequential agent execution with role labels\n- `skills`: Curated list of domain skills (loaded from `/skills/` directory)\n- `postTaskHooks`: Analysis/post-processing phases\n\n#### **Workflow Patterns**\n\nBoth profiles follow a **7-phase sequencing loop**:\n\n1. **Setup** → Read task metadata, acknowledge JIRA, set status to \"In Progress\"\n2. **Research** → Dispatch researcher/analyst; read status.json (only, never output.md)\n3. **Planning** → Dispatch planner; create task breakdown in `tasks.json`\n4. **Implementation** → Dispatch writer/coder for each planned task; track attempts/iterations\n5. **Review** → Dispatch reviewers in parallel; iterate if `needs-revision` (max 3 attempts)\n6. **Delivery** → Dispatch scribe; post comment, attach artifacts, update knowledge base\n7. **Exit** → Print `===RALPH_RESULT_START===` block with status\n\n**Iteration tracking:**\n- ralph-docs: `tasks.json` tracks per-task attempts (initial write = attempt 1, max 4 rounds = attempt 4)\n- ralph-vscode: `tasks.json` + planner verification pass; max 2-3 passes total\n\n#### **Naming Conventions**\n\n**Agents:**\n- Format: `ralph.{agent-name}.agent.md`\n- Examples: `ralph.ralph.agent.md`, `ralph-researcher.agent.md`, `ralph.malph.agent.md`\n\n**Artifacts:**\n- Workload root: `.ralph/tasks/{{ taskId }}/`\n- Artifact dir: `.ralph/tasks/{{ taskId }}/artifacts/{agent-name}/`\n- Control files: `state.md`, `tasks.json`, `status.json`, `output.md`\n\n**Commits:**\n- ralph-docs: `docs({{ taskId }}):`\n- ralph-vscode: `ralph/{{ taskId }}:`\n\n#### **status.json & Routing**\n\nEvery agent returns a minimal status contract:\n```json\n{\n  \"agent\": \"agent-name\",\n  \"task_id\": \"ISSUE-123\",\n  \"status\": \"completed|partial|blocked\",\n  \"result\": \"researched|analyzed|implemented|approved|delivered\",\n  \"summary\": \"Human-readable one-liner\",\n  \"artifacts\": [\"artifact-list\"],\n  \"next_hint\": \"optional guidance\",\n  \"iteration\": 1\n}\n```\n\n**Purity rule**: Orchestrator reads ONLY `status.json`, never artifact content. This keeps context tight and forces delegation.\n\n---\n\n### **2. FRACTAL-FACTORY PATTERN (Meta-Orchestrator)**\n\nThis is a **system-generation factory** that produces validated agent families from domain specifications. It's self-referential: fractal-factory itself follows the fractal pattern.\n\n#### **Agent Organization & Roles**\n\n**Three-tier hierarchy:**\n\n1. **Guide** (entry point, one-time)\n   - `fractal-factory-guide` — interviews user, builds context.json, invokes orchestrator\n\n2. **Orchestrator** (pipeline router)\n   - `fractal-factory` — routes 7-pass pipeline, handles re-entry from gap hunting\n\n3. **Coordinators** (pass managers, pure routers)\n   - `discovery-coordinator` → chains 4 discovery specialists\n   - `analysis-coordinator` → chains 3 analysis specialists\n   - `planning-coordinator` → chains 3 planning specialists\n   - `execution-coordinator` → chains 3 execution specialists (prompts + infra)\n   - `verification-coordinator` → chains 2 verification specialists\n   - `gap-hunting-coordinator` → runs gap hunter (re-entry decision maker)\n   - `delivery-coordinator` → chains 3 delivery specialists\n\n4. **Specialists** (leaf workers, substantive work)\n   - **Discovery** (Pass 1): domain-scanner, invariant-extractor, asset-auditor, exemplar-analyzer\n   - **Analysis** (Pass 2): pipeline-architect, artifact-designer, depth-analyzer\n   - **Planning** (Pass 3): roster-planner, routing-planner, test-planner\n   - **Execution** (Pass 4): prompt-writer ↔ prompt-reviewer (loop), infra-writer\n   - **Verification** (Pass 5): checklist-validator, audit-oracle\n   - **Gap Hunting** (Pass 6): gap-hunter (if dirty → re-enter Pass 2/3)\n   - **Delivery** (Pass 7): packager, documentation-writer, report-writer\n\n#### **No profile.json — Uses context.json & progress.json**\n\n**context.json** (user-filled, then read-only):\n```json\n{\n  \"domainName\": \"string\",\n  \"outputPath\": \"string\",\n  \"inputFiles\": {\n    \"domainBrief\": \"path\",\n    \"domainDocs\": \"path\",\n    \"invariants\": \"path\",\n    \"exemplars\": \"path\"\n  },\n  \"options\": {\n    \"maxGapCycles\": 3,\n    \"pipelinePasses\": [1,2,3,4,5,6,7]\n  },\n  \"target\": {\n    \"namingPrefix\": \"string\",\n    \"outputDirectory\": \"string\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**progress.json** (owned by orchestrator):\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"currentPass\": 0-7,\n  \"passes\": {\n    \"pass1_discovery\": { \"status\": \"pending|active|completed|re-entered\", ... },\n    \"pass2_analysis\": { ... },\n    ...\n  },\n  \"counts\": {\n    \"subdomainsDiscovered\": 0,\n    \"agentsPlanned\": 0,\n    \"agentsWritten\": 0,\n    \"agentsVerified\": 0\n  },\n  \"gapHunting\": {\n    \"cyclesCompleted\": 0,\n    \"maxCycles\": 3,\n    \"converged\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### **7-Pass Pipeline with Re-Entry**\n\n```\nPass 1: Discovery → discovery-coordinator\nPass 2: Analysis → analysis-coordinator\nPass 3: Planning → planning-coordinator\nPass 4: Execution → execution-coordinator (prompts + review loop)\nPass 5: Verification → verification-coordinator (checklist + oracle)\nPass 6: Gap Hunting → gap-hunting-coordinator\n    ├─ if gaps-found AND cycles < maxCycles → re-enter Pass 2 or 3\n    ├─ if converged → proceed to Pass 7\n    └─ if cycles >= maxCycles → forced proceed to Pass 7\nPass 7: Delivery → delivery-coordinator (package + docs + report)\n```\n\n**Re-entry logic:**\n1. Verification finds `gaps-found` → gap-hunter decides which pass to re-enter (2 or 3)\n2. Increment `gapHunting.cyclesCompleted`\n3. Reset all passes from re-entry point through pass 6 to `pending`\n4. Resume pipeline from reset pass\n5. Max 3 cycles; then forced proceed with gaps documented\n\n#### **Coordinator Purity Rule**\n\nEvery coordinator is a **pure router**:\n- Reads child `status.json` files\n- Dispatches children in sequence\n- Updates own `status.json`\n- **NEVER** reads child `output.md` or writes domain artifacts\n- **NEVER** does substantive work\n\n**Example routing table** (discovery-coordinator):\n```\nRead domain-scanner status.json\n  ├─ missing → Dispatch domain-scanner\n  ├─ result: scanned → Dispatch invariant-extractor\n  └─ result: insufficient-input → Write own status: blocked\n\nRead invariant-extractor status.json\n  ├─ missing → Dispatch invariant-extractor\n  ├─ result: extracted → Dispatch asset-auditor\n  └─ ...\n```\n\n#### **Specialist Execution Model**\n\nSpecialists do substantive work:\n- Read domain artifacts (`domain-model.json`, `context.json`, schemas)\n- Perform analysis/generation\n- Write to designated output files\n- Write own `status.json` with result codes\n- Prepend to `manifest.json`\n\n**Example: prompt-writer**\n- Reads: `roster.json`, `architecture.json`, `domain-model.json`, `test-plan.json`\n- Writes: `.agent.md` files for each agent (bottom-up order: specialists → coordinators → orchestrator)\n- Checks `status` field in roster; skips already-written agents\n\n#### **Produced Agent Prompt Schema**\n\nEvery agent `.agent.md` follows strict universal structure:\n\n```markdown\n---\ndescription: '...'\nmodel: claude-opus-4.6\nname: 'prefix-agent-name'\nuser-invocable: false  # true only for guide\n---\n\n# Agent Title\n\nYou are a **{role}** for the {domain} system.\n\nYou must never use `ask_questions` or request human input.\n\n## Context\nRead `.<domain>/context.json` for...\n\n## Inputs\n1. Artifact A\n2. Artifact B\n\n## Process  # Specialists\nOR\n## Purity Rule  # Coordinators\nOR\n## Pipeline Routing  # Orchestrator\n\n## Write Rules\nWrite to `.<domain>/agents/<name>/status.json` (schema)\nWrite to `.<domain>/agents/<name>/output.md`\nPrepend to `.<domain>/manifest.json`\n\n## Status Contract\n{ result codes and schema }\n```\n\n#### **Artifact Schemas**\n\nFractal factory defines schemas for:\n- `domain-model.schema.md` — domain structure (subdomains, invariants, dependencies)\n- `context.schema.md` — execution config\n- `progress.schema.md` — pipeline state\n- `produced-agent.schema.md` — agent prompt structure\n- And output artifacts: `roster.json`, `architecture.json`, `test-plan.json`, `manifest.json`\n\n#### **Bootstrap & Execution**\n\n1. **One-time setup:**\n   ```bash\n   bash fractal-factory-bootstrap.sh\n   # Creates .fractal-factory/ with initial progress.json, manifest.json, context.json template\n   ```\n\n2. **Invocation:**\n   - User invokes `@fractal-factory-guide` (one-time)\n   - Guide fills in `context.json`\n   - Guide invokes `fractal-factory` (orchestrator)\n   - Orchestrator runs 7-pass pipeline autonomously\n   - Gap hunting can re-enter passes 2-3 up to 3 times\n   - Delivery produces final output package\n\n---\n\n### **3. COMPARATIVE SUMMARY TABLE**\n\n| Aspect | Ralph-Profile | Fractal-Factory |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Route live JIRA/ADO tasks | Generate new agent families |\n| **Trigger** | JIRA comment (@Ralph) | User invokes guide (once) |\n| **Config** | `profile.json` (variant-based) | `context.json` (domain-specific) |\n| **State** | Per-task `.ralph/tasks/{{ taskId }}/` | Pipeline-wide `.fractal-factory/progress.json` |\n| **Hierarchy** | 2-tier (orchestrator + specialists) | 3-tier (guide → orchestrator → coordinators → specialists) |\n| **Agents** | Task-focused (researcher, writer, reviewer) | System-generation-focused (scanners, designers, writers) |\n| **Parallelism** | Within review phase (6 reviewers parallel) | Sequential pass chain; discovery specialists sequential |\n| **Iteration** | Task-level retries (max 3-4 per task) | Cycle-level re-entry (max 3 full cycles) |\n| **Purity** | Orchestrator reads `status.json` only | Coordinators + orchestrator read `status.json` only |\n| **Output** | JIRA comment, PR, pushed branch | Complete `.agent.md` family + schemas + tests |\n| **Status.json** | One per agent instance per task | One per agent per pass (reusable slots) |\n| **Naming** | `ralph.{agent}.agent.md` | `{prefix}-{agent}.agent.md` |\n| **Control Files** | `state.md`, `tasks.json`, `status.json` | `progress.json`, `roster.json`, `domain-model.json` |\n| **Re-entry** | Per-task reviewers (max 3 rounds) | Multi-pass cycles (max 3 cycles, re-entry pass 2-3) |\n\n---\n\n### **4. KEY PATTERNS EXTRACTED**\n\n#### **Ralph-Profile Common Patterns:**\n1. **Variant routing**: Match task metadata → select agent stage\n2. **Dispatch chain**: Researcher → Planner → Writer → Reviewers → Scribe\n3. **Status-based routing**: Read `status.json.result` to decide next step\n4. **Parallel review**: All reviewers dispatch simultaneously; iterate on rejection\n5. **Task breakup**: Planner converts research into `tasks.json`; writer executes per-task\n6. **Skill injection**: Each stage lists domain skills; agents load them on dispatch\n7. **Two-level delegation**: Orchestrator is pure router; all work is subagent work\n8. **Purity boundary**: Never read subagent output content; only status\n9. **Control file exception**: `tasks.json` is the only artifact orchestrator writes (besides its own status)\n\n#### **Fractal-Factory Common Patterns:**\n1. **Context-driven**: Single `context.json` drives the entire pipeline\n2. **Progress tracking**: `progress.json` owns all pass states and counts\n3. **Pass sequencing**: Ordered 1-7 passes; each pass has a coordinator\n4. **Bottom-up generation**: Write specialists first, then coordinators, then orchestrator\n5. **Purity at two levels**: Coordinators are pure routers; orchestrator is pure pass manager\n6. **Re-entry decision**: Gap hunter suggests which pass to re-enter; orchestrator decides\n7. **Schema-driven output**: All produced agents follow universal template + domain-specific content\n8. **Manifest prepending**: Every agent prepends execution record to manifest.json\n9. **Cycle counting**: Track gap-hunting cycles; force proceed after max cycles\n10. **Status contract consistency**: Every agent's `status.json` follows the same shape\n\n#### **Naming Conventions:**\n- **Ralph profiles**: `ralph.{agent-name}.agent.md`; commit prefix matches project (docs/ralph)\n- **Fractal factory**: `{namingPrefix}-{agent-name}.agent.md`; everything prefixed\n- **Workload paths**: `.ralph/tasks/{taskId}/artifacts/` vs `.fractal-factory/agents/`\n\n---\n\n### **5. Docker/Infrastructure Patterns**\n\nNeither profile.json nor fractal-factory explicitly defines Docker or Dockerfile patterns in their core files. However, both assume:\n- **Container execution**: Agents run in isolated containers with mounted artifact directories\n- **File-based IPC**: Status.json, output.md, and control files are the communication protocol\n- **MCP server sidecar**: Each execution has mcpServers (JIRA, ADO, web-fetch, custom tools)\n- **Resource mounting**: `.ralph/` or `.fractal-factory/` directories mounted as workspace volume\n\nDockerfile/compose patterns would be in the orchestration layer (beyond these profiles), not in the agent definitions themselves.\n\n---\n\nThis analysis captures the **two distinct exemplar families**: ralph-profiles are task-delivery routers with variant-based triggering, while fractal-factory is a self-referential system generator with pass-based orchestration and cycle-aware re-entry.",
