e system's phases, trace artifact flows between them, and decide whether a depth-2 or depth-3 hierarchy makes sense—this feeds into a populated architecture.json.\n\nPlanning comes next, where I'll coordinate the roster (up to 80 agents), routing tables for each coordinator, and golden test scenarios. Then execution focuses on writing and reviewing all the agent prompts for the romantic-fantasy-writer family. Verification follows to ensure everything works as intended. The domain's complexity—17 subdomains across creative production and quality gates—drives why discovery was intensive and why the resulting 9-phase pipeline with 7 cross-cutting concerns needs a deep hierarchy to manage. This cascades into planning challenges with 50-80 agents and intricate ordering dependencies, making execution the longest phase due to writer-reviewer loops and retries, while verification must validate 64 invariants and gap hunting becomes essential for catching conditional invariant gaps in the craft toolbox before final delivery of this large, interconnected system.\n\nFor re-entry, the gap hunter can trigger returns to analysis if missing insights emerge, to planning for roster gaps, or to execution for prompt issues, with verification failures also cycling back to execution. We're capped at 10 cycles with convergence signaled when no new items surface from gap hunting.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what each pass should document about the produced system so downstream agents have enough context to work effectively—the pipeline architect needs to capture the 9-phase structure and how the cross-cutting concerns thread through it so the artifact designer, depth analyzer, and roster planner can build on that foundation.\n\nWriting architecture.json and output files... Writing the pipeline configuration...",
