              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-pipeline-architect/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Pipeline Architect — Output Report\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nDesigned a 7-pass factory pipeline for producing the **romantic-fantasy-writer** agent family. All 7 universal passes are included because the domain scores HIGH on every complexity axis. The produced system is a 9-phase creative pipeline with 7 cross-cutting systems, requiring an estimated 50-70 agents across a depth-3 hierarchy.\\n\\n## Domain Characteristics Assessment\\n\\n| Dimension | Rating | Key Driver |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Discovery complexity | HIGH | 17 subdomains: 10 sequential creative phases + 7 cross-cutting concerns |\\n| Analysis depth | HIGH | 64 invariants including 14 conditional craft tools; series-first artifact design |\\n| Planning complexity | HIGH | ~60 agents with writer-reviewer loops, sequential chapter dispatch, parallel beta reading |\\n| Execution pattern | HIGH | Writer → adversarial auditor loops at every creative phase; 50-70 prompts to write |\\n| Verification needs | HIGH | 64 invariants to trace; conditional craft toolbox wiring; 7 cross-cutting systems |\\n| Gap-hunting value | HIGH | Conditional invariants + series lifecycle are easy to gap; expect 2-4 cycles |\\n\\n## Pipeline Pass Table\\n\\n| Pass | Name | Purpose | Agents | Parallelizable | Entry Condition | Exit Condition |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Discovery | Scan domain for subdomains, invariants, assets, patterns | 4 | No | context.json exists | domain-model.json fully populated |\\n| 2 | Analysis | Design pipeline, artifact schemas, hierarchy depth | 3 | Yes | Pass 1 complete | architecture.json fully populated |\\n| 3 | Planning | Design agent roster, routing tables, test scenarios | 3 | No | Pass 2 complete | roster.json + routing + test-plan.json populated |\\n| 4 | Execution | Write all agent prompts, review them, write infra | 3 (looping) | No | Pass 3 complete | All .agent.md files written and reviewed |\\n| 5 | Verification | Validate prompts against checklist and compliance audit | 2 | No | Pass 4 complete | Verification report produced |\\n| 6 | Gap Hunting | Adversarial search for missing coverage across 8 categories | 1 | N/A | Pass 5 complete | Clean (converged) or maxCycles reached |\\n| 7 | Delivery | Package, document, and report on the complete agent family | 3 | No | Pass 6 done | Output directory complete |\\n\\n**Total unique factory agents**: 19 (across 7 passes)\\n**Total produced agents (estimated)**: 50-70\\n\\n## Produced System Architecture Overview\\n\\nThe romantic-fantasy-writer system processes one story at a time through a 9-phase creative pipeline:\\n\\n### Creative Phase Pipeline\\n\\n```\\nUser Input → Guide → Orchestrator\\n  → Phase 1: Concept Development (writer + auditor loop)\\n  → Phase 2: World-Building (4+ specialist writers + auditor loop per subcategory)\\n  → Phase 3: Character Design (3+ specialist writers + auditor loop)\\n  → Phase 4: Plot Architecture (plotter + auditor loop)\\n  → Phase 5: Prose Style Development (analyzer + calibrator + auditor loop)\\n  → Phase 6: Chapter Drafting (sequential per-chapter: drafter + auditor loop, continuity update)\\n  → Phase 7: Revision (3 sequential passes: developmental → line → copy)\\n  → Phase 8: Beta Reading (5 parallel lenses → synthesis → re-entry to revision if needed)\\n  → Phase 9: Polish & Delivery (proofreader + summarizer + series KB updater)\\n→ Published output\\n```\\n\\n### Cross-Cutting Systems (woven into phases)\\n\\n1. **Adversarial Quality Gates** — writer→auditor loops at phases 1-6 with maxRetries\\n2. **Continuity Tracking** — updated after every chapter, cross-referenced by auditors\\n3. **Series Knowledge Management** — load for sequels, cross-reference during production, merge on completion\\n4. **Artifact Management** — subcategory decomposition enforcement, directory structure, cross-references\\n5. **Genre Compliance** — dual-arc enforcement, HFN/HEA resolution, fantasy-first plot\\n6. **Craft Toolbox Framework** — 14 conditional tools selected per-story, verified by auditors against profile\\n7. **Originality Assurance** — zero-tolerance plagiarism, transformative influence only\\n\\n## Re-Entry Rules\\n\\n| # | Trigger | Re-Entry Pass | Reset Passes | Max Re-Entries | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Architecture gaps (missing schemas, wrong depth) | 2 (Analysis) | 2-6 | 2 | Fundamental design issues cascade downstream |\\n| 2 | Roster/routing gaps (missing agents, unhandled result codes) | 3 (Planning) | 3-6 | 3 | Roster changes require prompt updates |\\n| 3 | Prompt-level gaps (missing invariant enforcement, wrong references) | 4 (Execution) | 4-6 | 5 | Most common gap type — prompt updates and re-review |\\n| 4 | Structural violations (pure-router breach, status contract issues) | 4 (Execution) | 4-5 | 2 | Prompt fixes without roster/routing changes |\\n\\n**Expected re-entry pattern**: Most findings will be Rule #3 (prompt-level). The conditional craft toolbox (14 tools needing per-story activation wiring) and series KB lifecycle (load→reference→merge) are the most common gap sources.\\n\\n## Convergence Strategy\\n\\n- **Max gap cycles**: 10 (from context.json)\\n- **Expected convergence**: 2-4 cycles\\n- **Convergence signal**: Gap hunter returns zero new items across all 8 search categories\\n- **Limit behavior**: Proceed to delivery with outstanding items documented as \\\"known gaps — recommended for manual review\\\"\\n- **Escalation signal**: If cycles 4+ still find pass 3 (routing) gaps, this indicates a deeper planning issue that should be flagged in the gap-hunting coordinator's status\\n\\n### Why 10 Cycles is Appropriate\\n\\nThe domain has 64 invariants, 7 cross-cutting systems, and a conditional craft toolbox creating a large surface area for gaps. However, most gaps will be prompt-level (Rule #3) and addressable in 1-2 cycles. The 10-cycle limit provides headroom for the rare case where a gap-hunting finding reveals a cascade (e.g., discovering that the series KB merge step was architecturally misplaced, requiring re-analysis → re-planning → re-execution → re-verification).\\n\\n## Passes Skipped\\n\\n**None.** All 7 universal passes are included. Domain analysis confirms:\\n- Discovery was essential (17 subdomains, 64 invariants to find)\\n- Analysis is essential (complex artifact schemas, clear depth-3 need)\\n- Planning is essential (60+ agents with complex routing)\\n- Execution is essential (the core work)\\n- Verification is essential (64 invariants to trace)\\n- Gap hunting is essential (conditional invariants + series lifecycle = high gap probability)\\n- Delivery is essential (complex output needs documentation)\\n\\n## Key Risks for Downstream Agents\\n\\n1. **Artifact designer**: The subcategory decomposition is deep (world split 6 ways, characters per-file, revision per-pass-per-chapter). The accumulator pattern from PATTERN-009 must be adapted for creative content, not just structured JSON.\\n\\n2. **Depth analyzer**: This should be a clear depth-3 decision. 17 subdomains × 47 estimated agents cannot be routed by a flat orchestrator. The hierarchy is: guide → orchestrator → ~10 phase coordinators → ~50 specialists.\\n\\n3. **Roster planner**: The hardest challenge will be the chapter-drafting phase. Each chapter needs sequential dispatch with continuity-tracker update between chapters. The coordinator must handle N chapters where N is determined at runtime (from the plot outline).\\n\\n4. **Routing planner**: Writer-reviewer loops appear in 6 of 9 phases. These must be templated rather than hand-crafted for each phase. The beta-to-revision re-entry loop is structurally different (parallel lenses → synthesis → conditional re-entry).\\n\\n5. **Prompt writer**: The 14 conditional craft tools (T1-T14) must be referenced in every adversarial auditor's checklist with the instruction \\\"verify against selected tools from Story Craft Profile only.\\\" Getting this wiring wrong is the #1 predicted gap-hunting finding.\\n\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
        "parsed": null
      }
    }
