              "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator && python3 << 'PYEOF'\\nimport json\\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\\n\\n# Load all inputs\\nwith open('.fractal-factory/architecture.json') as f:\\n    arch = json.load(f)\\nwith open('.fractal-factory/domain-model.json') as f:\\n    dm = json.load(f)\\nwith open('.fractal-factory/context.json') as f:\\n    ctx = json.load(f)\\n\\nmaxDepth = ctx['options']['maxDepth']  # 4\\nmaxAgents = ctx['options']['maxAgents']  # 80\\n\\noverview = arch['pipeline']['producedSystemOverview']\\nphases = overview['creativePhases']\\ncrosscutting = overview['crossCuttingSystems']\\nsd_map = {s['name']: s for s in dm['subdomains']}\\n\\n# ============================================================\\n# DEPTH ANALYSIS\\n# ============================================================\\n# The produced system: guide → orchestrator → phase coordinators → specialists\\n# Depth-2: coordinator → specialists directly\\n# Depth-3: coordinator → sub-coordinators → specialists\\n#\\n# Key insight: the \\\"orchestrator\\\" in the produced system routes to ~10 phase coordinators.\\n# That orchestrator itself is depth-2 if it has ≤8 direct children, depth-3 if >8.\\n# Each phase coordinator routes to its specialists.\\n#\\n# But per the task description, the hierarchy is already:\\n#   guide (depth 0) → orchestrator (depth 1) → coordinators (depth 2) → specialists (depth 3)\\n# So we're already at depth 3 baseline. The question is whether any coordinator needs\\n# sub-coordinators, making it depth 4 (which maxDepth=4 allows).\\n#\\n# Let me re-read the system prompt more carefully:\\n# \\\"depth-2 hierarchy (coordinator → specialists) or depth-3 (coordinator → sub-coordinators → specialists)\\\"\\n# This is about EACH coordinator's internal depth, not overall system depth.\\n\\n# The orchestrator routes to 9 creative phase coordinators + cross-cutting utility agents.\\n# That's potentially 10+ direct children for the orchestrator.\\n\\n# Per-coordinator analysis:\\n\\ndecisions = []\\n\\n# === ORCHESTRATOR (routes to all phase coordinators) ===\\n# The orchestrator has 9 phase coordinators + potentially cross-cutting agents as children.\\n# That's 9+ direct children. But the orchestrator is a sequential pipeline dispatcher,\\n# so it dispatches phases in order. This is manageable even with >8 children because\\n# the routing is strictly sequential (phase 1 → 2 → ... → 9).\\n# However, cross-cutting concerns (adversarial gates, continuity tracking, etc.) are\\n# embedded into phase agents, not separate agents at the orchestrator level.\\n# Decision: depth-2. The orchestrator dispatches 9 sequential phases + some utility.\\n# While >8 children, they're strictly sequential, simple routing.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-orchestrator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"top-level-orchestrator\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 10,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 7,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 9,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Orchestrator dispatches 9 creative phases sequentially plus a delivery/polish phase. Despite 10 direct children (exceeding the 8-specialist threshold), the routing is strictly sequential — each phase completes before the next starts, with no parallel dispatch needed. Cross-cutting concerns (adversarial gates, continuity, genre compliance) are embedded into phase-level agents, not dispatched separately from the orchestrator. Sequential pipeline routing with deterministic ordering keeps complexity manageable at depth-2.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 orchestrator + 10 coordinators dispatched sequentially\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 1: CONCEPT-DEVELOPMENT ===\\n# Agents: concept-writer + concept-auditor (writer-reviewer loop)\\n# Cross-cutting: 3 (genre-compliance, craft-toolbox, adversarial-gates — but these are\\n#   embedded into the auditor's prompt, not separate agents)\\n# Estimated specialists: 2 (writer + auditor)\\n# Simple sequential writer-reviewer loop.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-concept-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-1-concept\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Simple writer-reviewer loop with 2 specialists (concept-writer + concept-auditor). Well under the 8-specialist threshold. Cross-cutting concerns (genre compliance, craft toolbox selection, adversarial gating) are enforcement responsibilities embedded into the auditor's prompt, not separate agents.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 2 specialists = 3 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 2: WORLD-BUILDING ===\\n# 6 subcategory artifacts: geography, magic, politics, culture, history, flora/fauna\\n# Agents: world-builder (writes all 6 subcategories) + world-auditor\\n# But with 6 subcategories, we might need:\\n#   Option A: 1 writer for all 6, 1 auditor → 2 specialists (depth-2)\\n#   Option B: 6 writers (one per subcategory), 1 auditor → 7 specialists (depth-2)\\n#   Option C: 6 writers grouped into sub-coordinators + auditor → depth-3\\n# The domain model says est_agents=4, suggesting ~3 writers + 1 auditor or\\n# 1 writer + 1 auditor + continuity-tracker + series-kb interaction.\\n# With 6 subcategories but est 4 agents, likely 1 writer iterates through all 6.\\n# But the writer-reviewer loop means the coordinator dispatches writer → auditor → writer...\\n# Cross-cutting: 4 (continuity, series-kb, adversarial, artifact-mgmt)\\n# Let's estimate: 1 world-writer + 1 world-auditor + maybe 1 continuity-agent = 3-4 agents\\n# This is well under 8. Depth-2.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-worldbuilding-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-2-worldbuilding\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Despite 6 subcategorized artifacts (geography, magic, politics, culture, history, flora/fauna), the world-builder agent iterates through them sequentially in a single writer-reviewer loop. Estimated 4 specialists: world-writer (produces all 6 subcategory files), world-auditor (validates consistency, completeness, and genre compliance), plus continuity-tracker and series-kb agents. 4 specialists is well under the 8-specialist threshold. Sub-coordinators for each subcategory would add overhead without benefit since the artifacts must be internally consistent anyway.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 4 specialists = 5 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 3: CHARACTER-DESIGN ===\\n# Agents: character-writer + character-auditor + possible per-character iteration\\n# But character files are per-character, not fixed count at design time.\\n# Est 3 agents: character-designer + character-auditor + cast-index-manager\\n# Cross-cutting: 4 (genre, continuity, adversarial, series-kb)\\n# Under 8. Depth-2.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-character-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-3-character\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"3 specialists estimated: character-designer (creates all character profiles), character-auditor (validates depth, voice distinctness, genre compliance), cast-index-manager (maintains the cast index). The coordinator iterates per-character through the designer in a writer-reviewer loop, but this is sequential iteration within the same agents, not more children. 3 specialists is well under threshold.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 3 specialists = 4 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 4: PLOT-ARCHITECTURE ===\\n# Agents: plot-architect + plot-auditor + subplot-tracker-writer\\n# Output: structure-framework, chapter-outlines, tension-map, subplot-tracker (4 artifacts)\\n# Est 3 agents. Cross-cutting: 4.\\n# Under 8. Depth-2.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-4-plot\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"4 specialists estimated: plot-architect (structure framework + chapter outlines), tension-mapper (tension-map.json), subplot-tracker (subplot tracking), plot-auditor (validates dual-arc interleave, pacing, craft tool compliance). Despite 4 output artifacts and 4 cross-cutting concerns, the routing is a simple writer-reviewer loop. Well under 8-specialist threshold.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 4 specialists = 5 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 5: PROSE-STYLE-DEVELOPMENT ===\\n# Agents: style-analyst + style-auditor\\n# Est 2 agents. Simple phase.\\n# Depth-2.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-style-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-5-style\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Simplest creative phase. 2 specialists: style-analyst (produces style guide from reference fiction and concept), style-auditor (validates originality, completeness, voice calibration). Basic writer-reviewer loop. Well under all thresholds.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 2 specialists = 3 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 6: CHAPTER-DRAFTING ===\\n# This is the most complex phase by far:\\n# - Sequential per-chapter production (chapter N+1 can't start until N is done)\\n# - Writer-reviewer loop per chapter\\n# - Continuity tracking after every chapter\\n# - Cross-cutting: 4 (continuity, genre, adversarial, craft-toolbox)\\n# Agents: chapter-drafter + chapter-auditor + continuity-tracker + possibly scene-planner\\n# Est 4 agents from domain model.\\n# But the KEY complexity is sequential chapter iteration, not agent count.\\n# The coordinator dispatches the same agents repeatedly per chapter.\\n# Agent count ≤8. Depth-2.\\n# The routing complexity (per-chapter loop) is handled by coordinator logic, not hierarchy.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-drafting-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-6-drafting\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"4 specialists: chapter-drafter, chapter-auditor, continuity-tracker, scene-planner. Despite being the longest phase (sequential chapter production), the complexity is in iteration count, not agent hierarchy. The coordinator re-dispatches the same 4 agents per chapter in sequence. Adding sub-coordinators would slow per-chapter throughput without reducing complexity. 4 specialists is well under threshold. Routing complexity (per-chapter iteration + writer-reviewer inner loop) is handled by coordinator state machine logic.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 4 specialists = 5 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 7: REVISION-EDITING ===\\n# 3 sequential passes: developmental edit → line edit → copy edit\\n# Each pass is a separate specialist\\n# Agents: developmental-editor + line-editor + copy-editor + possibly revision-auditor\\n# Est 4 agents. Cross-cutting: 3.\\n# Under 8. Depth-2.\\n# Could argue for depth-3 with sub-coordinator per edit pass, but 3 passes is simple.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-revision-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-7-revision\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"4 specialists across 3 distinct edit passes: developmental-editor, line-editor, copy-editor, plus revision-synthesizer (aggregates findings). Despite 3 subdomain groups (the 3 edit passes), each pass is a single specialist with simple sequential dispatch. The coordinator runs: developmental → line → copy → synthesize. Sub-coordinators per edit pass would add 3 agents for no routing benefit since each 'pass' is one agent. 4 specialists is well under threshold.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 4 specialists = 5 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 8: BETA-READING-SIMULATION ===\\n# 5 parallel independent lenses + synthesizer\\n# Agents: romance-lens, fantasy-lens, craft-lens, sensitivity-lens, originality-lens + beta-synthesizer\\n# Est 6 agents. Cross-cutting: 3.\\n# Under 8 threshold but at 6 — still depth-2. All 5 lenses run in parallel.\\n# Depth-3 would only make sense if lenses were grouped, but they're all independent.\\n# Could add a lens-coordinator to fan out the 5 lenses, but that's the coordinator itself.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-beta-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-8-beta-reading\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"6 specialists: 5 independent beta-reader lenses (romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality) + 1 beta-synthesizer. The 5 lenses run in parallel — this is the only phase with parallel dispatch. Despite 6 specialists (highest count of any phase), they're all at the same level with identical routing patterns: coordinator fans out 5 lenses, waits for all, dispatches synthesizer. Sub-coordinators would add a grouping layer with no routing benefit since all lenses are independent and homogeneous.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 6 specialists = 7 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# === Phase 9: POLISH-DELIVERY ===\\n# Agents: proofreader + series-kb-updater\\n# Est 2 agents. Simple phase.\\n# Depth-2.\\n\\ndecisions.append({\\n    \\\"coordinator\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer-polish-coordinator\\\",\\n    \\\"role\\\": \\\"phase-9-polish\\\",\\n    \\\"depth\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"estimatedDirectChildren\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"estimatedTotalSpecialists\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"crossCuttingConcerns\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"subdomainGroups\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"3 specialists: proofreader (final copy check), series-kb-updater (merges book facts into series knowledge base), delivery-packager (chapter summaries + book metadata). Simple sequential dispatch. Well under all thresholds.\\\",\\n    \\\"subCoordinators\\\": None,\\n    \\\"budgetImpact\\\": \\\"1 coordinator + 3 specialists = 4 agents\\\"\\n})\\n\\n# ============================================================\\n# BUDGET CALCULATION\\n# ============================================================\\n# 1 guide\\n# 1 orchestrator\\n# 9 phase coordinators (concept, world, character, plot, style, drafting, revision, beta, polish)\\n# + their specialists\\n\\ntotal_guide = 1\\ntotal_orchestrator = 1\\ntotal_coordinators = 0\\ntotal_specialists = 0\\n\\nfor d in decisions:\\n    if d['role'] == 'top-level-orchestrator':\\n        pass  # already counted\\n    else:\\n        total_coordinators += 1\\n        total_specialists += d['estimatedTotalSpecialists']\\n\\ntotal = total_guide + total_orchestrator + total_coordinators + total_specialists\\n\\nprint(f\\\"\\\\n=== BUDGET ===\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Guide: {total_guide}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Orchestrator: {total_orchestrator}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Phase coordinators: {total_coordinators}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Total specialists: {total_specialists}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"TOTAL: {total}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Budget: {maxAgents}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Status: {'within-limit' if total <= maxAgents else 'OVER BUDGET'}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Remaining capacity: {maxAgents - total}\\\")\\n\\n# Cross-cutting systems may add agents beyond phase specialists\\n# Let's account for possible standalone utility agents:\\n# - Continuity-tracker: already counted in drafting phase\\n# - Series-KB-manager: counted in polish phase\\n# - Adversarial auditors: embedded in each phase's auditor\\n# Some cross-cutting agents might be shared across phases rather than duplicated.\\n# But the domain model already accounted for these in the per-subdomain estimates.\\n\\nPYEOF\\n\", \"description\": \"Full depth analysis calculation\", \"initial_wait\": 15}"
