        "reasoning_text": "The analysis shows 43 agents total, well within the 80 budget with 37 remaining. All phase coordinators are depth-2 since no single coordinator exceeds 8 specialists. \n\nHowever, I should think more carefully about this. The domain model estimates 47 total agents from subdomains, and the pipeline architect estimated 50-70 agents. My count of 43 seems low. Let me reconsider:\n\n1. The cross-cutting subdomains (adversarial-quality-gates: 4, continuity-tracking: 2, series-knowledge-management: 3, artifact-management: 2, genre-compliance: 2, craft-toolbox-framework: 2, originality-assurance: 1) add 16 more agents that aren't necessarily all embedded into phase agents.\n\n\n2. For the depth analysis, I'm really just determining hierarchy depth per coordinator rather than building the full roster—that's the roster-planner's job. I need to estimate accurately enough for budget purposes though.\n3. Some cross-cutting agents could be standalone shared resources that multiple phase coordinators dispatch to, like a continuity-tracker or series-kb-manager, while others might get duplicated across phases or sit under a shared cross-cutting coordinator depending on the agent-as-function pattern.\n4. The adversarial-quality-gates subdomain's 4 estimated agents are already counted as per-phase auditors, and the continuity-tracking subdomain's 2 agents include the continuity-tracker I already accounted for under phase 6.\n5. The orchestrator dispatching 9 sequential phase coordinators is a manageable depth-2 pattern—similar to the fractal factory's orchestrator dispatching 7 passes—so I don't need to push it to depth-3.\n6. My current count sits at 43 agents against the pipeline architect's 50-70 estimate, so I should review whether some phases need more agents than I initially estimated.\n\nNow I'm recognizing that some cross-cutting concerns genuinely need dedicated agents rather than just being embedded in prompts. The continuity-tracker and series-KB manager are good examples—they're reused across multiple phases and might sit directly under the orchestrator or under a utility coordinator. I should reconsider which subdomains warrant their own agent teams versus which can be handled through prompt engineering, and then reassess the depth structure accordingly. Looking at the rest of the coordinator hierarchy, I'm seeing the style-auditor, drafting-coordinator through polish-coordinator phases, which brings the total to around 43 agents across the system. I think that's a solid baseline—the roster planner can refine it further. Since no single coordinator manages more than 8 specialists, I'm leaning toward keeping most at depth-2 rather than pushing for depth-3 across the board.\n\nThe overall system architecture works out to a four-level hierarchy: the guide at the top, then the orchestrator, phase coordinators, and specialists below. That gives us a depth of 3, which fits comfortably within the maxDepth constraint of 4. I'm ready to write this up now.",
