      'gen_ai.tool.call.arguments': `{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-depth-analyzer/output.md","file_text":"# Depth Analysis — romantic-fantasy-writer Agent Family\\n\\n**Agent**: fractal-factory-depth-analyzer  \\n**Task**: pass2/depth-analysis  \\n**Date**: 2026-03-15  \\n**Result**: analyzed — all coordinators depth-2, 43 estimated agents within 80-agent budget\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nThe romantic-fantasy-writer system uses a **uniform depth-2 hierarchy** for all 10 coordinators (1 orchestrator + 9 phase coordinators). Despite the domain's substantial complexity — 17 subdomains, 64 invariants, 9 sequential creative phases, and 7 cross-cutting systems — no single coordinator requires more than 6 direct specialist children, keeping all well under the 8-specialist threshold for depth-3 escalation.\\n\\nThe overall system depth is **3 levels**: guide → orchestrator → phase coordinator → specialist. This is within the maxDepth=4 limit, leaving room for depth-3 escalation if the roster planner determines any coordinator needs it.\\n\\n**Budget**: 43 of 80 agents (54%), leaving 37 slots for the roster planner to expand.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Decision Methodology\\n\\n### Thresholds Applied\\n\\n| Criterion | Depth-2 | Depth-3 Trigger |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Direct specialists | ≤ 8 | > 8 |\\n| Cross-cutting concerns | ≤ 3 | > 3 |\\n| Distinct subdomain groups | ≤ 3 | > 3 |\\n| Exemplar pattern suggests depth-3 | No | Yes |\\n\\n**Decision rule**: If ANY criterion triggers AND maxDepth allows it, recommend depth-3. However, criterion override is permitted when the exceeding factor is **enforcement-only** (rules embedded in prompts, not separate routing targets) or when the routing pattern is **strictly sequential** (no parallel dispatch complexity).\\n\\n### Key Insight: Cross-cutting ≠ Separate Agents\\n\\nSeveral coordinators exceed the cross-cutting concerns threshold (>3), but in every case, the cross-cutting concerns are **enforcement responsibilities embedded into auditor prompts** rather than separate agents requiring independent routing. For example:\\n\\n- Genre compliance → rules in every auditor's validation checklist\\n- Craft toolbox enforcement → conditional checks referencing Story Craft Profile\\n- Adversarial quality gates → the writer-reviewer loop pattern itself\\n- Continuity tracking → tracked by a dedicated agent within the drafting phase\\n\\nThis distinction is critical: 4 cross-cutting concerns that are prompt instructions ≠ 4 additional agents requiring routing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Per-Coordinator Decision Table\\n\\n| # | Coordinator | Phase | Depth | Specialists | Cross-Cut | Subdomain Groups | Criteria Exceeded | Override? |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | orchestrator | top-level | 2 | 10 children | 7 | 9 | specialist, cross-cut, subdomain | Yes — sequential pipeline |\\n| 2 | concept-coordinator | Phase 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 1 | none | — |\\n| 3 | worldbuilding-coordinator | Phase 2 | 2 | 4 | 4 | 1 | cross-cut | Yes — enforcement-only |\\n| 4 | character-coordinator | Phase 3 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 1 | cross-cut | Yes — enforcement-only |\\n| 5 | plot-coordinator | Phase 4 | 2 | 4 | 4 | 1 | cross-cut | Yes — enforcement-only |\\n| 6 | style-coordinator | Phase 5 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 1 | none | — |\\n| 7 | drafting-coordinator | Phase 6 | 2 | 4 | 4 | 1 | cross-cut | Yes — iteration-driven |\\n| 8 | revision-coordinator | Phase 7 | 2 | 4 | 3 | 3 | none | — |\\n| 9 | beta-coordinator | Phase 8 | 2 | 6 | 3 | 1 | none | — |\\n| 10 | polish-coordinator | Phase 9 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 1 | none | — |\\n\\n### Override Justifications\\n\\n**Orchestrator (3 criteria exceeded)**: Children are coordinators, not specialists. The routing is strictly sequential: phase 1 → 2 → ... → 9, identical to the exemplar fractal-factory orchestrator with 7 sequential passes. Cross-cutting concerns are distributed into phase-level agent prompts, not dispatched separately.\\n\\n**Worldbuilding, Character, Plot, Drafting coordinators (cross-cutting exceeded)**: In all cases, the 4 cross-cutting concerns (continuity, genre, adversarial, series-KB/craft-toolbox) are enforcement rules embedded in auditor prompts, not separate routing targets. The auditor agent checks these concerns during its review pass.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Specialist Breakdown Per Coordinator\\n\\n### Phase 1: Concept Development (2 specialists)\\n- **concept-writer** — distills raw inputs into story concept, premise, craft profile\\n- **concept-auditor** — validates genre promise, completeness, craft tool selection\\n\\n### Phase 2: World-Building (4 specialists)\\n- **world-builder** — produces 6 subcategory files (geography, magic, politics, culture, history, flora/fauna)\\n- **world-auditor** — cross-references subcategories for consistency, validates completeness\\n- **continuity-initializer** — seeds the continuity tracker from worldbuilding artifacts\\n- **series-kb-reader** — loads existing series canon for sequel consistency\\n\\n### Phase 3: Character Design (3 specialists)\\n- **character-designer** — creates per-character profiles + cast index\\n- **character-auditor** — validates voice distinctness, agency, psychological depth\\n- **series-character-loader** — loads returning characters from series KB\\n\\n### Phase 4: Plot Architecture (4 specialists)\\n- **plot-architect** — structure framework + chapter outlines\\n- **tension-mapper** — tension map with dual-arc pacing validation\\n- **subplot-tracker** — subplot lifecycle management\\n- **plot-auditor** — validates completeness, craft tool compliance, dual-arc interleave\\n\\n### Phase 5: Prose Style (2 specialists)\\n- **style-analyst** — analyzes reference fiction, produces style guide\\n- **style-auditor** — validates originality, voice calibration, POV variations\\n\\n### Phase 6: Chapter Drafting (4 specialists)\\n- **scene-planner** — pre-chapter scene breakdown from outline\\n- **chapter-drafter** — writes chapter prose\\n- **chapter-auditor** — adversarial review per chapter\\n- **continuity-tracker** — updates continuity state after each chapter\\n\\n### Phase 7: Revision Editing (4 specialists)\\n- **developmental-editor** — plot holes, pacing, structure issues\\n- **line-editor** — prose quality, voice, show-don't-tell violations\\n- **copy-editor** — grammar, spelling, consistency\\n- **revision-synthesizer** — aggregates findings into prioritized revision list\\n\\n### Phase 8: Beta Reading (6 specialists)\\n- **romance-lens** — emotional resonance, chemistry, relationship pacing\\n- **fantasy-lens** — world immersion, magic consistency, plot engagement\\n- **craft-lens** — prose quality, structure compliance, craft tool adherence\\n- **sensitivity-lens** — cultural representation, power dynamics, consent\\n- **originality-lens** — plagiarism detection, derivative passages\\n- **beta-synthesizer** — aggregates all 5 lens findings into prioritized action items\\n\\n### Phase 9: Polish & Delivery (3 specialists)\\n- **proofreader** — final copy polish incorporating beta/revision findings\\n- **series-kb-updater** — merges completed book into series knowledge base\\n- **delivery-packager** — chapter summaries, book metadata, final packaging\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Budget Impact Analysis\\n\\n| Component | Count |\\n|---|---|\\n| Guide | 1 |\\n| Orchestrator | 1 |\\n| Phase coordinators | 9 |\\n| Total specialists | 32 |\\n| **TOTAL** | **43** |\\n| Budget (maxAgents) | 80 |\\n| Remaining capacity | 37 (46%) |\\n\\n### Capacity Notes\\n\\nThe 37-slot remaining capacity gives the roster planner significant room to:\\n\\n1. **Add dedicated cross-cutting utility agents** — if enforcement-only embedding proves insufficient, the roster planner can create shared utility agents (e.g., dedicated genre-compliance-checker, craft-tool-validator) without triggering depth-3.\\n\\n2. **Split complex specialists** — if any single agent's prompt grows too large (e.g., world-builder handling 6 subcategories), it can be split into per-subcategory specialists while staying under the 8-specialist threshold.\\n\\n3. **Add per-chapter variants** — the drafting phase might benefit from specialized agents for action scenes vs. romance scenes vs. exposition, if the roster planner determines the generic chapter-drafter is too broad.\\n\\n4. **Create parallel infrastructure** — some cross-cutting tasks (series KB operations, artifact validation) might warrant their own coordinator if they grow beyond what fits in individual phase agents.\\n\\n### No Budget Reductions Needed\\n\\nNo depth-3 decisions were made, so no depth-3-to-depth-2 reductions were necessary. If any phase later escalates to depth-3, the budget impact would be:\\n- Adding 2-3 sub-coordinators per depth-3 phase\\n- Each sub-coordinator adds 1 agent to the count\\n- Even 3 phases at depth-3 (adding ~9 sub-coordinators) would only bring total to ~52, still well under 80\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Depth-3 Escalation Watch List\\n\\nThree coordinators are flagged for monitoring during roster planning:\\n\\n### 1. Beta-Reading Coordinator (6 specialists, headroom: 2)\\n**Trigger**: If the roster planner splits any lens into sub-lenses (e.g., romance-lens → tropes-lens + chemistry-lens + pacing-lens).  \\n**Proposed sub-coordinators**: content-lenses (romance, fantasy) + quality-lenses (craft, sensitivity, originality).\\n\\n### 2. Drafting Coordinator (4 specialists, headroom: 4)\\n**Trigger**: If per-chapter validation is decomposed into separate specialist agents (voice-checker, pacing-validator, craft-tool-auditor) rather than embedded in chapter-auditor.  \\n**Proposed sub-coordinators**: chapter-production (scene-planner, chapter-drafter) + chapter-validation (chapter-auditor, continuity-tracker, additional checkers).\\n\\n### 3. Worldbuilding Coordinator (4 specialists, headroom: 4)\\n**Trigger**: If the roster planner creates per-subcategory specialist writers instead of one world-builder iterating through all 6 subcategories.  \\n**Proposed sub-coordinators**: physical-world (geography, flora/fauna) + social-world (politics, culture, history) + magical-world (magic system).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Relations`... 1615 more characters,
