ns\\n\\nNow I\'m breaking down the major subdomains that structure this work. The pipeline phases form the backbone: user guidance and configuration, concept distillation, world building (the most complex domain requiring specialists across geography, magic, politics, culture, and flora/fauna), character design with protagonist and romance arcs, plot architecture, scene execution, and quality assurance. Each domain has distinct complexity levels and specialist requirements, ranging from single contributors for simpler phases to teams of 3-4 for intricate world-building work.\\n\\nCharacter design spans protagonist arcs, romance, supporting cast, antagonists, voice guides, and relationship mapping—requiring 2-3 specialists. Plot architecture handles structure selection, chapter outlines, tension mapping, and subplot interleaving at similar complexity. Chapter drafting is the production engine, demanding 2-4 specialists for writing, continuity, and segmentation. Revision and editing involves developmental, line, and copy editing phases with high complexity requiring multiple editors.\\n\\nBeta reading simulation brings in five specialized reader perspectives—romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, and originality—each providing distinct feedback. Polish and delivery wraps up with final proofread and package assembly. The system also includes adversarial consistency auditing across every creative phase with 6+ auditor specializations to catch inconsistencies, and genre compliance checking for romantic fantasy rules.\\n\\nContinuity and knowledge management tracks cross-chapter consistency, character knowledge, promises to readers, and timelines. Series architecture handles the knowledge base lifecycle and cross-book consistency for sequel support. Artifact management organizes all the subcategories and cross-references, while the craft toolbox and quality framework...\\n\\nNow I\'m reconsidering the structure itself. The pipeline phases should be the core subdomains, and the cross-cutting concerns shouldn\'t be treated as separate phases since they span across multiple parts of the process. Let me reorganize around the actual workflow: user interaction handles the guide agent and input gathering, concept development comes next...\\n\\nDefining the major pipeline phases...\\n\\nThe chapter drafting phase needs about 4 agents to handle the sequential writing while maintaining continuity. Then I\'m mapping out the revision and editing workflow—developmental, line, and copy editing all handled by a dedicated team. For quality assurance, I\'m planning multi-lens beta reading that covers romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, and originality angles, plus adversarial auditors at each phase to challenge consistency and cut unnecessary elements. I also need continuity tracking across chapters and books, along with series knowledge management to handle cross-book consistency and sequel planning.\\n\\nFor the remaining infrastructure, I\'m setting up artifact management for directory structure and naming conventions, genre compliance to enforce romantic fantasy rules and heat levels, a craft toolbox framework that lets me select quality tools per story, and an originality audit to catch plagiarism and ensure transformative influence. Adding up the agent counts across all these systems, I\'m looking at roughly 36 agents total to run the full pipeline. I\'m verifying that Prose Style Development is genuinely its own phase distinct from drafting—and yes, the brief explicitly calls it out as Phase 5, so that confirms my 17 subdomains are the right level of granularity. Now I can finalize the output."},{"type":"text","content":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the entire domain. Let me write the outputs."}],"finish_reason":"error"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01WkMVNokyqAi4pnnnVST8yx","name":"report_intent","arguments":{"intent":"Writing domain model outputs"}}],"finish_reason":"error"},{"role":"assistant","pa