      'gen_ai.input.messages': '[{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_01UtGrQ1MhdKQVp47hkbjqYX","response":"Path does not exist"}]},{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_011Qdt845xHhUwT8MQxM35F4","response":"docs\\nvscode"}]},{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_01RKh7Yx5ccAGmVkrxLTk8Ez","response":"1. # Run Analyzer\\n2. \\n3. You analyze agent execution quality to identify issues, failure patterns, and improvement opportunities. You operate in one of two modes:\\n4. \\n5. 1. **Per-subagent mode** (normal) — Dispatched once per subagent found during a run. The orchestrator tells you which subagent to analyze and where the mapper\'s extraction file is.\\n6. 2. **Infrastructure failure mode** — Dispatched when the CLI crashed before any subagent could execute (no mapper data, no subagent spans). You analyze the infrastructure failure itself.\\n7. \\n8. Use the **agent-eval** skill for evaluation dimensions and scoring guidance. Load the **cli-debug-log-analysis** skill if you need to drill deeper into the raw cli-debug.log beyond what the mapper extracted.\\n9. \\n10. {% render \'agent-as-function-contract\' %}\\n11. \\n12. ### Result codes\\n13. \\n14. | Code | Meaning |\\n15. |------|---------|\\n16. | `analyzed` | Analysis complete, report written |\\n17. | `skipped` | Nothing to analyze (missing extraction or artifacts, and no infrastructure failure detected) |\\n18. \\n19. ## Input\\n20. \\n21. The main pipeline just finished processing work item **{{ taskId }}** (\\"{{ taskTitle }}\\").\\n22. \\n23. ### Per-subagent dispatch\\n24. \\n25. The orchestrator dispatches you with context specifying:\\n26. - **Target subagent name** — the subagent you are analyzing (or `\\"infrastructure\\"` for infra-failure mode)\\n27. - **Mapper extraction file path** — structured data extracted by `subagent-mapper` (may be absent for infra failures)\\n28. - **Output directory** — where to write your analysis (namespaced by target subagent)\\n29. \\n30. Read the mapper extraction file first — it contains the subagent\'s span boundaries, tool call sequence, token metrics, errors, and artifact data.\\n31. \\n32. ### Log directory (fallback)\\n33. \\n34. If the mapper extraction is insufficient, raw logs are at: `{{ hook.taskOutputDir }}`\\n35. \\n36. Load the **cli-debug-log-analysis** skill for parsing recipes. Use the span boundaries from the mapper extraction to target your `sed`/`grep` commands.\\n37. \\n38. ### Artifact directory\\n39. \\n40. Subagent output artifacts: `{{ hook.taskOutputDir }}/*-artifacts/`"}]},{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_011WncKUvJfq2MUqJhDYpv83","response":"1. # Agent Improver\\n2. \\n3. You improve Ralph agent templates, skills, shared includes, and MCP server configurations based on a **single subagent\'s** execution analysis. You are dispatched once per subagent — the orchestrator tells you which subagent to improve and where the analysis report is.\\n4. \\n5. {% render \'agent-as-function-contract\' %}\\n6. \\n7. ### Result codes\\n8. \\n9. | Code | Meaning |\\n10. |------|---------|\\n11. | `improved` | Changes applied based on analysis findings |\\n12. | `no-action` | Analysis had no actionable findings |\\n13. \\n14. ## Input\\n15. \\n16. The Run Analyzer has analyzed one subagent from the execution of work item **{{ taskId }}** (\\"{{ taskTitle }}\\").\\n17. \\n18. ### Per-subagent dispatch\\n19. \\n20. The orchestrator dispatches you with context specifying:\\n21. - **Target subagent name** — the subagent whose analysis you are acting on\\n22. - **Analysis file path** — the run-analyzer\'s per-subagent report\\n23. - **Output directory** — where to write your improvement summary (namespaced by target subagent)\\n24. \\n25. Read the analysis report at the path provided. If the file doesn\'t exist or the analyzer\'s result was `skipped`, write your own status as `no-action` and stop.\\n26. \\n27. All your changes must be grounded in specific findings from the analysis.\\n28. \\n29. ## Scope of Changes\\n30. \\n31. You may modify files in these directories:\\n32. \\n33. | Directory | What lives there |\\n34. |---|---|\\n35. | `profiles/*/agents/` | Agent templates (Liquid `.agent.md` files) |\\n36. | `shared/agent-includes/` | Shared Liquid partials |\\n37. | `shared/skills/` | Agent skill definitions — use the **skill-creator** skill if improving or creating new skills. |\\n38. | `shared/mcp-servers/` | MCP server manifests and custom server code — use the **mcp-builder** skill if making changes to or adding new MCP servers. |\\n39. | `.github/instructions/` | Codebase instruction files |\\n40. | `.github/skills/` | Copilot workspace skills |"}]},{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_013An12cWYUVax5FuxvnaJJL","response":"1. ## Artifact Contract\\n2. \\n3. You are a **subagent** in a multi-agent pipeline. You communicate results through the **filesystem**, not through conversation. The orchestrator that dispatched you will only read your `status.json` — never your output artifacts.\\n4. \\n5. ### Artifact directory\\n6. {% raw %}\\n7. Your artifact directory is: `{{ artifactDir }}/{{ agentName }}/`\\n8. \\n9. Create it if it doesn\'t exist. Write all output files here.\\n10. {% endraw %}\\n11. \\n12. ### Required files\\n13. \\n14. **1. Primary artifact** — your main output:\\n15. {% raw %}\\n16. - Non-iterative agents: `{{ artifactDir }}/{{ agentName }}/output.md`\\n17. - Iterative agents (writer, coder, reviewer, validator): `{{ artifactDir }}/{{ agentName }}/output-v{N}.md` where N is your **cumulative dispatch count** (monotonically increasing across all dispatches for this task, regardless of which planned subtask each dispatch handles)\\n18. - **Agents with custom artifact lists:** If your prompt defines specific named output files (e.g., `handoff.md`, `jira-comment.md`), those replace `output.md` as your primary artifacts. List all of them in your `status.json` `artifacts` array. You do not need to also produce `output.md`.\\n19. {% endraw %}\\n20. \\n21. ⚠️ **Determining N:** Before writing your output file, list existing `output-v*.md` files in your artifact directory. Set N = highest existing number + 1. If no files exist, N = 1. **Never reset N** when switching between planned subtasks — the version sequence must be continuous across all dispatches.\\n22. \\n23. **2. status.json** — structured status the orchestrator reads for routing:\\n24. {% raw %}\\n25. ```json\\n26. {\\n27.   \\"agent\\": \\"{{ agentName }}\\",\\n28.   \\"task_id\\": \\"{{ taskId }}\\",\\n29.   \\"status\\": \\"completed\\",\\n30.   \\"result\\": \\"<your result code>\\",\\n31.   \\"summary\\": \\"<one line, max ~100 tokens>\\",\\n32.   \\"artifacts\\": [\\"{{ agentName }}/output.md\\"],\\n33.   \\"next_hint\\": \\"<suggested next agent or null>\\",\\n34.   \\"iteration\\": 1\\n35. }\\n36. ```\\n37. \\n38. Write to: `{{ artifactDir }}/{{ agentName }}/status.json`\\n39. {% endraw %}\\n40. \\n41. | Field | Description |\\n42. |---|---|\\n43. | `status` | `completed` · `failed` · `blocked` — did you finish? |\\n44. | `result` | Your task-specific outcome code (e.g. `analyzed`, `implemented`, `pass`, `fail`) |\\n45. | `task_id` | Always the **work item ID** (e.g., `DOC-3189`) from `{{ taskId }}` — never a subtask ID like `TASK-01` |\\n46. | `summary` | Enough for a routing decision. Not a report. |\\n47. | `artifacts` | Paths relative to the artifact root — **must list ALL output files** written across all iterations, not just the latest |\\n48. | `next_hint` | Suggested next agent. Orchestrator can override. |\\n49. | `iteration` | Cumulative dispatch count for this task (monotonically increasing — if the orchestrator dispatches you 3 times, your 3rd dispatch writes `iteration: 3` regardless of which subtask each dispatch handled) |\\n50. \\n51. **3. manifest.json** — **REQUIRED**: append an entry to the shared audit log:\\n52. {% raw %}\\n53. Read `{{ artifactDir }}/manifest.json`. If it doesn\'t exist, create it as `[]`. Append your entry and write it back. **You must do this after writing status.json — it is not optional.**\\n54. \\n55. ⚠️ **Timestamp must be real.** Run `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` and use the output — never guess or hardcode a date.\\n56. \\n57. ```json\\n58. {\\n59.   \\"timestamp\\": \\"<run date command above>\\",\\n60.   \\"agent\\": \\"{{ agentName }}\\",\\n61.   \\"artifacts\\": [\\"{{ agentName }}/output.md\\"],\\n62.   \\"status\\": \\"completed\\",\\n63.   \\"result\\": \\"<your result code>\\",\\n64.   \\"iteration\\": 1\\n65. }\\n66. ```\\n67. \\n68. ⚠️ **Iterative agents:** The `artifacts` array in your manifest entry must list **all artifacts written across all iterations** — not just the current iteration\'s file. For example, if this is your 2nd dispatch, list both `output-v1.md` and `output-v2.md`. This matches the cumulative listing rule for `status.json`.\\n69. {% endraw %}\\n70. \\n71. ### Completion Sequence\\n72. \\n73. After finishing your primary work, always execute this sequence:\\n74. \\n75. 1. Write your primary artifact (`output.md` or `output-v{N}.md`)\\n76. 2. Write `status.json`\\n77. 3. Read `manifest.json`, append your entry, write it back\\n78. \\n79. ### Reading upstream artifacts\\n80. \\n81. Read artifacts from other subagents directly from the filesystem. The orchestrator does not relay content between subagents. Look for upstream artifacts at:\\n82. {% raw %}\\n83. `{{ artifactDir }}/{upstream-agent-name}/output.md` (or `output-v{N}.md` for versioned artifacts)\\n84. {% endraw %}\\n85. \\n86. ### Control-file exception\\n87. \\n88. Some workflows define explicit **control files** such as `state.md` or `tasks.json`.\\n89. \\n90. These files are an exception to the normal routing rule:\\n91. - The orchestrator still routes on subagent `status.json` results\\n92. - The orchestrator may also read or update designated control files for phase, task-lifecycle, or per-task-attempt bookkeeping\\n93. - Subagents may read those control files only when their prompt explicitly tells them to\\n94. \\n95. Never use another subagent\'s narrative artifact (`output.md`, `output-v{N}.md`) as a substitu'... 21732 more characters,
