pipeline** (Concept → World Building → Character Design → Plot Architecture → Prose Style → Drafting → Revision → Beta Reading → Polish/Delivery) with **7 cross-cutting concerns** that span multiple phases.\\n8. \\n9. Supporting invariants document (`.fractal-factory/invariants.md`) provides 28 non-negotiable invariants and 14 craft toolbox techniques.\\n10. \\n11. ## Subdomain Table\\n12. \\n13. | ID | Name | Complexity | Est. Agents | Description |\\n14. |----|------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\\n15. | SD-001 | user-interaction | low | 1 | Guide agent: gathers inputs, validates, writes story-config.json, launches pipeline |\\n16. | SD-002 | concept-development | medium | 2 | Idea distillation, theme extraction, premise refinement, comp titles, craft profile |\\n17. | SD-003 | world-building | high | 4 | Geography, magic systems, politics, history, culture, flora/fauna, consistency bible |\\n18. | SD-004 | character-design | high | 3 | Protagonist profiles, romance arc, supporting cast, antagonists, voice guides, relationship web |\\n19. | SD-005 | plot-architecture | high | 3 | Story structure, chapter outlines, tension mapping, subplot tracking, dual-arc interleave |\\n20. | SD-006 | prose-style-development | medium | 2 | Voice calibration, style guide, tone palette, reference material analysis |\\n21. | SD-007 | chapter-drafting | high | 4 | Sequential chapter production with voice/style/continuity adherence, chapter hooks |\\n22. | SD-008 | revision-editing | high | 4 | Three-pass editing: developmental, line edit, copy edit, with full revision traceability |\\n23. | SD-009 | beta-reading-simulation | high | 6 | Five independent reviewer lenses: romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality |\\n24. | SD-010 | polish-delivery | medium | 2 | Final proofread, chapter summaries, series KB update, delivery packaging |\\n25. | SD-011 | adversarial-quality-gates | high | 4 | Per-phase adversarial auditors with kill-your-darlings mandate, severity-gated blocking |\\n26. | SD-012 | continuity-tracking | medium | 2 | Character locations, timeline, knowledge matrix, Chekhov\'s guns, cross-chapter facts |\\n27. | SD-013 | series-knowledge-management | high | 3 | Series KB lifecycle, book→series promotion, sequel contracts, cross-book consistency |\\n28. | SD-014 | artifact-management | medium | 2 | Subcategory decomposition, directory schema, per-book isolation, cross-references |\\n29. | SD-015 | genre-compliance | medium | 2 | Romantic fantasy rules, slow-burn pacing, HEA/HFN, trope execution, heat-level |\\n30. | SD-016 | craft-toolbox-framework | medium | 2 | Two-tier quality system, story craft profile selection, per-story tool adjustments |\\n31. | SD-017 | originality-assurance | low | 1 | Plagiarism prevention, transformative-only influence, originality self-audit |\\n32. \\n33. ## Cross-Cutting Concerns\\n34. \\n35. Seven subdomains are identified as cross-cutting — they interact with multiple pipeline phases rather than being confined to a single phase:\\n36. \\n37. 1. **Adversarial Quality Gates (SD-011)** — the most pervasive cross-cutting concern. Every creative phase (SD-002 through SD-007) must include an adversarial auditor as a sub-phase before completion. These auditors cross-reference against ALL prior phase artifacts, not just the current phase\'s output.\\n38. \\n39. 2. **Continuity Tracking (SD-012)** — spans drafting (updated after every chapter), revision (verified against), beta reading (craft lens checks), and series management (extends across books).\\n40. \\n41. 3. **Series Knowledge Management (SD-013)** — affects concept development (sequel loads series KB), all creative phases (cross-reference against series KB for sequels), polish/delivery (promotes new facts into series KB), and artifact management (directory schema).\\n42. \\n43. 4. **Artifact Management (SD-014)** — affects every phase that produces output. Enforces subcategory decomposition, per-book directory isolation, and cross-referencing conventions.\\n44. \\n45. 5. **Genre Compliance (SD-015)** — affects concept (genre promise), character design (both leads need agency), plot architecture (dual-arc interleave), drafting (heat-level adherence), and beta reading (romance reader lens).\\n46. \\n47. 6. **Craft Toolbox Framework (SD-016)** — selected during concept/plotting, then enforced by adversarial auditors in all subsequent phases.\\n48. \\n49. 7. **Originality Assurance (SD-017)** — affects prose style development (transformative-only influence from reference material), drafting (no plagiarism in prose), and beta reading (originality reader lens).\\n50. "}]},{"role":"tool","parts":[{"type":"tool_call_response","id":"toolu_01XvkQPnL3mZLDtav3pZGMvp","response":"1. # Asset Auditor Report — Romantic Fantasy Writer\\n2. \\n3. **Agent:** fractal-factory-asset-auditor  \\n4. **Iteration:** 1  \\n5. **Timestamp:** 2026-03-15T20:15:00Z\\n6. \\n7. ## Executive Summary\\n8. \\n9. Scanned the ralph-orchestrator repository for assets reusable by the romantic-fantasy-writer agent system. Examined **6 shared skill categories** (~50 skills), **9 MCP servers**, **7 agent include files**, **2 personality templates**, **4 post-hook patterns**, **2 agent profiles**, **28 fractal factory exemplar agents**, and **6 hook scripts**.\\n10. \\n11. **Result:** 25 assets catalogued as relevant. The vast majority of code-specific skills (TypeScript, Ruby, Jekyll, .NET, VSCode) are irrelevant to creative fiction writing. However, the **infrastructure patterns** — artifact handoff contracts, workflow phasing, multi-reviewer panels, revision loops, personality templates — are highly transferable.\\n12. \\n13. ## Scan Statistics\\n14. \\n15. | Category Scanned | Items Found | Relevant | Irrelevant |\\n16. |---|---|---|---|\\n17. | Shared Skills (domain/) | 20 | 3 | 17 |\\n18. | Shared Skills (integrations/) | 6 | 0 | 6 |\\n19. | Shared Skills (tasks/) | 6 | 3 | 3 |\\n20. | Shared Skills (workflow/) | 15 | 5 | 10 |\\n